WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.710
COLLEEN MCGINNESS:
--and board members.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:05.610
A 1997 graduate, Kerry has
been a member of the alumni

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.320
executive board for six years.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.340
She has served as secretary,
vice president, and now

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.420
president.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.180
On behalf of the Alumni
Board and the entire Alumni

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.930
Association, I wish to thank
her for her loyal service.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.690
She's done an outstanding job
leading the Alumni Association

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.355
Board during a difficult time.

00:00:23.355 --> 00:00:25.230
She's been the right
person in the right seat

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.750
to keep our volunteers engaged.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.450
Kerry and her husband Richard
reside in Glenview, Illinois.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.380
They have four grown children
and three grandchildren.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.430
Please join me in
welcoming Kerry.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.420
KERRY CUMMINGS:
Thank you, Colleen.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.210
It is my privilege to
introduce Dr. Larry

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.934
Arnn, who is in his 21st year
as president of Hillsdale.

00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:04.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.210
During this time,
Dr. Arnn has overseen

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.940
the unprecedented
growth of the college

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.030
both in the physical plant
and in the development

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.420
of its academic and
outreach efforts.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.630
The college has emerged
in the last 21 years

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.800
as truly a nationally ranked
undergraduate institution

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.670
with over 70% of students now
enrolling from out of state

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.690
and a near 20%
rate of admission.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.540
Under Dr. Arnn's
leadership, millions

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.840
are now able to access
Hillsdale's classical liberal

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.530
arts education through the
development of the college's

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.840
vast outreach efforts.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.120
These include the
Van Andel Graduate

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.600
School of Statesmanship,
Van Andel Graduate School

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.130
of Government Hillsdale in
DC, the Churchill Project,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.000
the Halter Shooting Sports
Center, the Gordon Biology

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.800
Station, 30 online
courses, the establishment

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.300
of Hillsdale K-12 with
40 affiliated schools

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.729
across the country,
the new Blake

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.760
Center for Faith and
Freedom, and the growth

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.380
of the college's flagship
publication, Imprimus,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.210
to more than 6 million readers.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.440
Dr. Arnn has published widely
on the issues of public policy

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.490
and political theory, is past
president of the Philadelphia

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.890
Society, is a
member of the board

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.230
of trustees for the
Heritage Foundation

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.380
and the board of directors
of the Henry Salvatore

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.950
Center of Claremont McKenna
College, the Intercollegiate

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.100
Studies Institute, and
the Claremont Institute.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.400
In 2015, Dr. Arnn
received the Bradley Prize

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.550
from the Lindy and Harry
Bradley Foundation.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:55.980
Recently, he was appointed to
former President Trump's 1776

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.560
Commission, which
was established

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.270
to restore in American education
an understanding of the history

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:06.930
and the principles of the
founding of the United States.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.300
Published widely in
newspapers and magazines

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.540
and periodicals on issues
of public policy, history,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.660
and political theory,
Dr. Arnn is the author

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.420
of three books,
Liberty and Learning,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.890
the Evolution of the American
Education, The Founders' Key,

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.630
the Divine and
Natural Connection

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.690
between the Declaration
and the Constitution

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:32.310
and What We Risk By Losing It,
and Churchill's Trial, Winston

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.120
Churchill and the Salvation
of Free Government.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.660
Dr. Arnn travels tirelessly
to educate others and raise

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.090
support for the
college, yet he still

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.850
finds time to
frequent the cafeteria

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.180
and have lunch with students
as well as teach classes

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.460
in politics and history.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.010
He and his wife Penny
have four children--

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.380
Henry, Katie, Alice, and Tony--

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.130
and recently welcomed their
first grandchild Charlotte.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.670
Please join me in welcoming
our dedicated and accomplished

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.310
president, Dr. Larry Arnn.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.304
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.910
LARRY ARNN: Neal Cole,
are you not a Sigma Chi?

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.980
Are you a Sigma Chi?

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.560
And he's skipping the
40th birthday party.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.410
Well done.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:44.130
Welcome back.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.005
Thank you, Colleen,
and thank you, Kerry.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.060
It's a joy to see you all.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:58.810
It's a measure of the continuity
of the college to have you all.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.960
The college is continuous.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.540
It's looks like we're going
to get to have college again

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.095
next year.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.530
I recognize two new--

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:13.730
Bill Nolan is not here.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:14.810
He's with the Sigma Chis.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:15.860
Are you here, Bill Nolan?

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.030
No.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:19.700
There's three new
trustees, and two are here.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.130
And I'm going to ask
them to stand up.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:25.700
Bill-- forget Bill
Nolan, he didn't come.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.340
Donna Acton and
David Huff are here.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.299
And would they stand up please?

00:05:30.299 --> 00:05:39.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.870
And I recognize the recipients.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.801
Two of them I know well.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:45.801
Two of them are too old.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.030
I'll prove how well
I know them and then

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:56.930
I'll praise the other two.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:00.500
Jared Veldheer is the best
football player we ever had.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.860
The second best is John
Sweeney, who's here tonight--

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.950
--whom we learned today is the
first academic All-American

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:14.840
in Hillsdale College history.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.730
And nobody mentioned
at the board meeting

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.560
because somebody
brought that up that he

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.780
was both kinds of All-American.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.900
And I don't know if Jared
was both kinds or not.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:31.000
Jared was like John,
an offensive lineman.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.590
And John and I revel in the fact
that on the Wonderlic IQ test

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:38.680
the offensive linemen
are the smartest.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.755
And I was an offensive
lineman, I just

00:06:41.755 --> 00:06:43.255
wasn't very good
at being a lineman.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.350
And there are two games
that mark in my memory

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.120
the excellence of the man.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.940
And one was we beat
Grand Valley State, won

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.020
the conference that year, and
we beat them with a long drive

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.020
where we ran over and
over on the left side.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.880
And because I was a
lineman and because I

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.490
love football in the
frustrated way somebody

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:10.620
loves it who was never
all that good at it,

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:15.480
I watched him block the left
side of the line of Grand

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:16.980
Valley State.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.240
It was just awesome.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.150
And they have a
very fancy scheme.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.510
And the linebackers are
fast and they move around.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.740
And the linemen stop things
up and then they dart in.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.370
Well, he just blocked
the guy in front of him,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.470
and the linebacker, too.

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.540
And he did it over and over.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.510
It was obvious, right?

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.430
And then we played
in the playoffs

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:45.410
the first game, Mankato State,
and when the offensive line

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:49.620
came out they didn't
look like oxen,

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.310
they looked like
mineral deposits.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.930
They were enormous.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:57.990
And the way they did it,
you can see, by the way,

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.240
that my method, my
approach to football

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.830
was to try to think it through.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:04.200
And so I noticed things, right?

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.980
And they would all start
in a similar direction

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.250
and they'd try to get momentum
and they'd try to run over us.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:15.810
And I said to Jared after the
game, how did you stop them?

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.910
And you know that was
his cue to say, well,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:21.510
we worked really hard and all
that stuff, and we trained

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:22.088
or something.

00:08:22.088 --> 00:08:23.880
That's what they say
in all the interviews.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:24.750
No, he didn't.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:28.230
He said, you take this foot
and you move it right over here

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:29.730
and then you go like this.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:34.900
He was giving me instruction how
to play in the offensive line.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:36.580
And the last thing
about him is when

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.809
he joined the Oakland Raiders
there were articles about him.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:43.809
And the articles always
mentioned something

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:48.400
about what a nice and
well-spoken young man he was.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.810
And this is the NFL,
they're not used to that.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:56.410
And the Raiders least of all.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:59.810
So I'm proud of him.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:04.430
Joy Pullmann is a journalist
and I had her husband in class.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:05.420
I never had her.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.340
She was too good for me.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:12.980
And she's won national fame
and she's a very fierce woman.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:18.110
And she's insightful,
and she's tough,

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:21.740
and she loves her
family and the family,

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:27.380
and she loves good
education and her education.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:31.380
And she writes about those
things with real conviction,

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.090
so I'm proud of her.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.560
Oh, Ron Gladnick.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.990
Where's Gladnick?

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:49.640
Yeah, so I met him
through John Servini.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:54.260
And they're just alike
except I think Gladnick may

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.165
be richer and Servini smarter.

00:09:59.735 --> 00:10:00.860
And what are you doing now?

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.410
Yeah, you sold your company
and got a lot of dough.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.080
And he's just agreed to
give a very large gift

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:11.100
to Hillsdale College--

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.270
or has he?

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.620
[LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE]

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.740
I did that on behalf of John.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:27.960
[LAUGHS] Congratulations to you.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.290
And he was a big jock and
he looks like it, too.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:34.250
He was really great.

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:35.030
OK, come on.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:35.810
Where else am I?

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.830
Our last recipient,
I don't know him.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.800
And where is he?

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:46.320
Right here.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.288
And he's a very successful man.

00:10:50.288 --> 00:10:51.830
And I looked up some
stuff about him,

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:56.930
and the people who worked with
and for him like him a lot.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:59.390
And he's going to give
a great talk tonight.

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:00.900
I look forward to that.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.240
And because of that, I'll
shut up pretty quick.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:07.320
I just want to tell
you, Hillsdale College

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:11.870
has never been more threatened
and never been more successful.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.210
Things are underway
in Washington D.C.

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:17.150
that will shift yet
more from the private

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:18.950
to the public sector.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.600
They're very interested
in controlling

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.790
charitable giving these days.

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:26.720
And surely that will not
be allowed to happen,

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:30.880
but if it is, surely we will
find a way to survive that.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:33.720
But in the face
of all that, it's

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:39.450
just marvelous to us
what's happened here.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:45.730
I'm not going to state numbers,
but giving to Hillsdale College

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:49.520
has risen every year
for 21 years in a row.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.680
But there have
been two big jumps,

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:53.870
both in the middle of a crisis.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:58.200
And after the first crisis it
didn't fall off after that.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.710
So I'm hoping, because
this jump is bigger

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:04.090
and I'm hoping it doesn't
fall off after this jump,

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:08.480
and that makes amazing
things possible.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.090
It basically means
that we can pay

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:15.340
for the excellence around here.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.640
We hired 21 new faculty
members this year.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.440
We set out to get 16, and the
applications were so great

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.940
that we would hire two where
we were only meant to hire one,

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:26.725
and I'm the one who
got carried away.

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:34.330
People from the Ivy League are
begging to come to work here.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:35.260
And why?

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:39.880
Because the kids are smart,
and they're respectful,

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:42.520
and they do the
work, and everybody

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:44.600
is nice to each other.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:48.930
In other words, it
acts like a college.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.900
And if somebody's
not nice to somebody,

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:53.080
then we'll speak to them.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:56.480
But that hardly ever happens.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.770
The entering scores
of the freshman class,

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:09.330
which is 75 students over
budget, that's 20% over budget,

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.090
are the highest
ever, and one point

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:14.820
below the averages of Harvard.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.430
And we could match
Harvard if we wanted to

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.370
if we just adopted the
practice of admitting everybody

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:22.980
who makes a perfect score
on the entrance exams.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:25.170
But we always turn
down six or eight or 10

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.900
of those, 10 in the
last year, and we do it

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:31.620
because they come in here acting
like they're God's gift to man.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:36.220
And we remind them that there
are only 17 or 18 years old.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.770
My favorite question is,
what can you possibly know?

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.690
And you know we
really pat ourselves

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:50.630
on the back about
things like that.

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:52.850
The college is successful.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.640
And the college is
reaching everywhere.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.395
That's an element
of its success.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:05.840
We have 20 charter schools,
we have 11 in process.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:09.440
We're getting ready to try
to form 50 in Tennessee

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:12.440
at the request of the
governor of Tennessee.

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:15.260
And to pursue that idea--

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.870
I mean he called me up and said
you're coming to Nashville.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:18.590
And I said, I am?

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:19.710
And he said, come see me.

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:21.170
So I go.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:24.710
He gives us lunch,
and he said a prayer.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:26.430
He had his whole senior
management there,

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:29.780
including one kid named
Gursky who's a Hillsdale kid--

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:30.740
Kelvin be quiet.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.530
After he said his
prayer he said,

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:41.130
we need Hillsdale
College in Tennessee.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:43.765
And I said, Governor, Hillsdale
College is very heavy and hard

00:14:43.765 --> 00:14:44.265
to move.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:50.190
And he said, I know.

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:53.520
But he said, you're starting
these education centers.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:57.120
We have one in D.C. that's a
graduate school and thriving

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:59.370
and we have one in Somers,
Connecticut, that has

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:01.840
a replica of Monticello on it.

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:04.260
And we know a million
people on our mailing list

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:07.650
who live within a 90-minute
drive at that place.

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.470
So we're going to
have stuff there,

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:11.210
and we're going to teach.

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:14.360
And there's about to be one, I
do believe, in Placer County,

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:17.390
California, North of Sacramento.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.760
And this morning's
news is that there

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.880
might be another one outside
Fresno on the King River

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:25.460
where it comes out
of the Sierras.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:27.940
Very beautiful place.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:30.370
And that's all
stupid and difficult,

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:33.890
but those things bring a
lot of money with them.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:37.780
And so I'm not trained
in being a businessman,

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.280
but I know enough
about it to know

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:41.290
you don't take your
existing revenues

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:44.020
and spread them over
a larger expense base.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:47.760
So we don't have to.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:49.820
Why are those things happening?

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:53.000
Oh, online courses.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:57.140
Half a million people new
signed up for those last year.

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:59.810
And there are 3
million total now.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:03.740
And the percentage that finished
the courses and take the exams

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:06.440
is rising all the time.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:13.010
I get stopped in airports now
saying, I'm taking your course.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:17.600
And then sometimes they're
70, sometimes they're 30,

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:21.530
sometimes they're 15, and
they act like students.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:25.140
They often have a question,
they make a comment.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:27.260
And if you ask them if
they're taking their exams

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:30.800
and how they're doing,
they turn into freshmen.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.930
They look at their feet
and look embarrassed.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.340
These are treasures.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:43.750
And they're happening here.

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:45.320
And it's unprecedented.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.860
I don't think that's
an exaggeration.

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:51.425
It's important for us to
remember who owns the college.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:56.830
Have you ever asked
yourself that question?

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.500
Who owns the college?

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:02.725
General Motors belongs to
its stockholders, right?

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:08.970
Hillsdale College
is 177 years old,

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:13.660
and it's been under the same
ownership all that time.

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:16.329
The owners are named in the
beautiful founding document

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:18.640
of the college, as
they are, by the way,

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:23.329
in the founding of every
charity in the nation.

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:28.000
The college belongs to its
beneficiaries, the people

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.690
it names in its founding
document, in its charter

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:33.265
that it intends to benefit.

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:40.290
Who are those people?

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.060
Every student.

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:45.970
But every student
down through time.

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:53.060
That means, we pray, millions
unborn, every employee,

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:55.380
of course.

00:17:55.380 --> 00:18:03.830
We serve it and we get
paid, but we owe it, too.

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:07.610
That means all the
citizens whose freedom

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:10.730
is to be reinforced by the
teachings of the college.

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:13.100
This is all said in
the founding document.

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:16.370
Every Christian who
wants to practice

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:20.280
an intelligent form
of piety, the college

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:23.010
belongs to all of them.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:24.930
The reason the board of
trustees is important

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:29.310
is because their charge
is to act for all of them,

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:31.140
to keep them in mind every day.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:33.540
We who run the college, we
try to keep them in mind, too,

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:37.090
but some days it's an emergency.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:38.242
There are 1,600 kids here.

00:18:38.242 --> 00:18:40.200
God knows what they're
going to do on any given

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:43.090
day, although they
behave better than you

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:44.170
did when you were here.

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:53.140
This bunch-- I feel really old--
this bunch sitting right here I

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.390
remember them distinctly
when they were students.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.536
And I'm astonished that
they're still alive.

00:18:58.536 --> 00:19:02.175
SPEAKER 1: They got married.

00:19:02.175 --> 00:19:03.050
LARRY ARNN: They did.

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:04.040
They got married.

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:11.510
And like Jody Lindt, he was a
boy given to self-reflection.

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:12.600
He's a classics major.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:15.017
He's actually very intelligent,
although you can't tell it

00:19:15.017 --> 00:19:16.880
by looking at him.

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:19.490
And he would come
in my office often,

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:22.160
and he would agonize
about whether he's

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:23.540
doing the right thing or not.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:25.370
He's a classics major.

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:27.530
The specific
purpose of the major

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:29.825
is to answer the question,
what is the right thing?

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:35.670
Ridiculous.

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:41.750
The Articles of
Association say that we

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:45.570
are going to perpetuate
the blessings

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:49.200
of civil and religious
liberty and intelligent piety.

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.750
The claim is that
those are eternal goods

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:57.120
and they are worthy of
eternal preservation.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:00.810
Who's responsible to do that?

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:04.770
Come to find out,
above all it is we

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:08.460
who have enjoyed its benefits.

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:12.150
When you were students here,
you paid a small fraction

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.600
of the cost of it.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:16.360
But that's not the cost.

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:20.740
The cost is work and
thinking and conversation

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:23.920
and concentration and alertness.

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:26.620
And a commitment, if
you study the things

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:29.230
we teach here, to keep
thinking about those

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:31.540
for the rest of your life.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:32.910
So what?

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:35.910
So you can live them and
teach them to others.

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:43.770
This year, we just
got our audit today,

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:46.730
and this year the
students have paid

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:50.520
for 6% of the cost
of Hillsdale College.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:54.000
And that's right,
actually, as long

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.270
as they remember to
be grateful for that.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:01.740
Because the funds have
been supplied by literally

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:06.000
millions of people
who get nothing for it

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:10.980
except a share in the pursuit
of the love that we represent

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:14.030
and share here actively.

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:17.600
Hillsdale College is
a massive community.

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:20.840
It encompasses people
by the tens of millions

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:23.660
in every corner of the land.

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:28.410
And millions of people
pin their hopes on it.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:31.080
We are the ones who have
received its benefits the most

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:33.760
richly.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:40.600
We are the ones who owe those
who haven't, who never get to.

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:44.850
I remember one year, there
were stirrings around

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:49.530
because a CCA audience
now from outside

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:52.820
is like 600 or 800 people.

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:56.930
And the people who
come are very annoying.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:21:58.460
They asked dumb questions.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:04.910
And we built this
auditorium and this

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:07.410
so they can have a place to sit.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:13.630
And a very nice
young woman, an RA,

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:15.850
when I talk every
time at the beginning

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:17.530
of every term to
the RAs, and that's

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:20.350
always an inspiring experience
because they're the best.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.980
And this young woman said to
me two years ago she said--

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:25.360
and I was about to leave.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:27.020
I said, anything else?

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:31.110
And I had picked up my
coat and my briefcase,

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:37.440
and she said, why do we have
to have these visitors here?

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:40.140
Isn't this for us?

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:42.360
And the record shows
that I put my coat down

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:47.290
and my briefcase and I answered
that question for 30 minutes.

00:22:47.290 --> 00:22:51.190
And I explained to her about
the obligation she has,

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:54.820
because she could never
pay for this you see,

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:57.160
and she has to eat,
and has to live,

00:22:57.160 --> 00:22:58.960
and has to have shelter
and all that stuff,

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:03.020
and everybody around
here has got to be paid.

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:07.390
And I said, do you see
the obligation you have?

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:10.660
They come here to do
a little bit of what

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:14.000
you get to immerse yourself in.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.360
Don't you owe them?

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:19.390
You should teach them.

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:22.420
And I know they
ask dumb questions

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:24.560
because they're not like us.

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:26.620
They're not in the military.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:30.670
They're civilians and
we're Marines here, right?

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:36.620
We train, we get ready,
we concentrate for years.

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:40.070
I say, when they
ask a dumb question,

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:44.090
go introduce yourself
and teach them something.

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:47.820
Help them understand.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:50.190
You want to live
in a free country?

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.250
That understanding
standing needs to grow

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:56.370
and it needs to grow fast.

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:59.230
And it all starts right here.

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:00.940
Thank you for coming back.

00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:02.900
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:18.310
KERRY CUMMINGS:
Thank you Dr. Arnn.

00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:20.920
The Hillsdale College
Alumni Association

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:24.190
was organized at the
close of the Civil War.

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:25.480
Think about that.

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:28.480
Alumni back then
saw the importance

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:30.910
of supporting the college.

00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:33.040
Upon its founding,
the Association

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.060
adopted its own
mission statement,

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:39.430
which called upon its members to
keep the alumni fully informed,

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:42.220
help maintain the
standards of the college,

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:44.770
aid in the enrollment
of new students,

00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:47.710
and encourage all alumni
to tangibly support

00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:49.930
the college by
developing programs

00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:53.320
to ensure its continued growth.

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:55.870
That is our same mission today.

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:59.020
Today, our 30-member
Alumni Association Board

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:03.940
works hard on behalf of the more
than 15,000 Alumni Association

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:09.340
members to honor and uphold
this 152-year-old commitment.

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:11.260
For 70 years, it's
been a tradition

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:14.170
to hold this banquet to
recognize the achievement

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:17.530
and service of some of our
most outstanding graduates,

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:20.740
and that's what brings
us here tonight.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:24.070
But before we transition
to honoring those awardees,

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:26.710
I'd like to take a few minutes
to acknowledge and thank

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:28.960
members of the Alumni
Association Board

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:31.820
who are finishing their
final year of service.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:35.530
These members all have
served two four-year terms

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:38.050
and have dedicated
their time and expertise

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:40.640
to the college in
a variety of ways.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.730
We're very grateful
for their service

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:46.630
and for their love and
enthusiasm for our alma mater.

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:49.660
The following members were not
able to join us this evening--

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:54.250
Tyler Horning, Scott Kenan,
Mark Donovan and Andrew Fink.

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:58.240
But I would like to call
those two members up here,

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:04.300
Maryann Ratoli, class of 1995,
and Richard Rockett Kyocera,

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:06.307
of class of 1976.

00:26:06.307 --> 00:26:08.140
I'd like you to come
up so I can present you

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:10.240
with a gift on
behalf of the college

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:13.580
and formally thank you for your
service to Hillsdale College.

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:16.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:34.950
On behalf of
Hillsdale College, we

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:37.350
want to thank you
for your service

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:40.320
by presenting you with this
beautiful watercolor of Central

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:40.830
Hall.

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:43.812
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:07.930
Well, it looks like I
may have moved forward

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:11.080
a little bit faster
than I meant to.

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:16.180
Let's begin this evening
will be recognizing

00:27:16.180 --> 00:27:18.160
four Outstanding Alumni.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:21.250
Their achievements are
detailed in your table programs

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:23.290
and I encourage
you to read those,

00:27:23.290 --> 00:27:25.300
if you have not
already done so, I'll

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:28.090
make an introduction of
each honoree that will

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:30.610
be followed by a short video.

00:27:30.610 --> 00:27:33.080
These videos have been
added in the last few years,

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:36.460
and I think they're just
fabulous ways of presenting

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:39.370
our honorees to all of you.

00:27:39.370 --> 00:27:42.280
After that, Dr. Arnn and I
will present each recipient

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:44.500
with an award,
and each recipient

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:47.840
will be asked to make
remarks if they wish.

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:49.900
And Jared, I know you
already volunteered

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:52.900
to make those remarks, so you're
going to be kicking this off.

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:55.750
He wanted you to know right
away what number he was,

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:59.770
and I said, well, we're starting
with you so you set that tone.

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:04.750
Our first honoree is Jared
Veldheer, class of 2009.

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:06.460
Jared is a retired--

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.710
Oh, his table would like to
applaud at the beginning.

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:11.770
Thank you.

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:14.830
Again, he's setting the tone.

00:28:14.830 --> 00:28:17.500
Jared is a retired
NFL offensive tackle

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:19.930
and was a four-year
starter at Hillsdale.

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:25.900
He was named First Team
All GLEAC in 2008 and 2009

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:29.170
and First Team All-American by
the American Football Coaches

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:31.780
Association in 2009.

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:35.710
He helped Hillsdale qualify
for the NCAA Division II

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:38.740
playoffs for the
first time in 2009

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:40.930
and advanced to
the second round.

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:44.170
In his 46 Hillsdale
starts at left tackle,

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:47.590
he never allowed a sack and
the Chargers had their two

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:49.750
highest-scoring seasons
up until that point

00:28:49.750 --> 00:28:53.080
in history 2007 and 2009.

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:56.350
Jared was a third-round draft
pick in the Oakland Raiders

00:28:56.350 --> 00:29:00.460
69th overall in
the 2010 NFL draft.

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:04.090
In March 2014 he signed
a five-year contract

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:05.860
with the Arizona Cardinals.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:09.880
In 2015, the Cardinals
finished with a 13 and 3 record

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:13.150
and had the highest-scoring
offense in the NFL.

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:16.330
Jared was named team
MVP by his teammates

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:19.240
after that 2014 season.

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:21.760
He left the Cardinals
in 2017 and went on

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.310
to play with the Denver
Broncos, the New England

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:28.030
Patriots, the Green Bay Packers,
and the Indianapolis Colts

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:31.870
before retiring from
the NFL in 2021.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:35.440
Jared is a native of Grand
Rapids, majored in biology,

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:38.620
and belonged to the Delta
Sigma Phi fraternity.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.350
He now serves as Director
of Health and Wellness

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:45.983
for Strategies Wealth Advisors
and is an active craft beer

00:29:45.983 --> 00:29:46.483
brewer.

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:51.790
He says thank you for that.

00:29:51.790 --> 00:29:54.280
His wife, Morgan,
also a 2012 graduate,

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:57.250
are active volunteers
and generous supporters

00:29:57.250 --> 00:29:58.810
of the college in many ways.

00:29:58.810 --> 00:30:01.690
They have two children,
Eva and Edwin,

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:03.610
and please give your
attention to the screen

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:06.580
to meet our distinguished
alumni honoree, Jared Veldheer.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.723
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:14.723 --> 00:30:16.390
JARED VELDHEER: As
an offensive lineman,

00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:18.057
you're always working
behind the scenes.

00:30:18.057 --> 00:30:22.570
You know how to
handle success humbly,

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:24.100
because when you
have that success,

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:26.680
your name's not going to be
printed in a newspaper article.

00:30:26.680 --> 00:30:29.320
Maybe you get a shout
out, the whole line

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:31.690
is a unit would get a shout
out from the running backs,

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:33.050
quarterbacks, coaches, whatever.

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:35.830
But we knew to never get
too high off our success,

00:30:35.830 --> 00:30:39.400
never get too low if there is
any kind of failure, that it

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:42.910
just really took five guys
to grind day in, day out

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:45.400
and look to keep getting
better and build as a unit.

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.460
My name is Jared Veldheer.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:52.830
I grew up in Grand Rapids,
Michigan, born and raised.

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:54.990
Football, even
professional football,

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:57.630
I didn't really see
that in my future much.

00:30:57.630 --> 00:31:00.990
Like maybe college football, but
I liked basketball a lot more.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:04.565
Coach Otterbein came and watched
one of my basketball games

00:31:04.565 --> 00:31:05.940
and then offered
me a scholarship

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:09.172
to play football after and
I accepted pretty quick

00:31:09.172 --> 00:31:11.130
because I was already
familiar with the school.

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:13.260
My brother went there and
played basketball there.

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:17.940
I could see the value in
being at an institution

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:19.695
like that for four-plus years.

00:31:22.250 --> 00:31:27.480
The NFL thought first popped in
my head after my freshman year.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:30.830
I was able to put on some
good size, strength, and speed

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:33.410
and a couple of guys just
started joking about it like,

00:31:33.410 --> 00:31:36.560
hey, you're just as big as these
guys that are getting drafted

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:38.720
or in the combine and
they're playing in the NFL.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:40.370
And it never seemed
too stressful

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:43.070
and it just seemed kind
of a lighthearted fun goal

00:31:43.070 --> 00:31:44.210
to go about.

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:47.750
We finished our football
season in November

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:49.790
and then immediately
I signed with an agent

00:31:49.790 --> 00:31:52.460
and that whole
process started to go.

00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:54.560
Heard my name called
with the 69th pick

00:31:54.560 --> 00:32:00.040
in the draft, which
was the third round.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:03.300
Just kind of a whirlwind,
just instant elation and then

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:04.500
just craziness.

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:12.000
Looking back at the
transition between Division II

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.820
and the NFL, getting moved from
left tackle the right tackle

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:16.878
probably sticks out is
the biggest challenge

00:32:16.878 --> 00:32:19.170
because I played left tackle
my whole time at Hillsdale

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:21.780
and played left tackle for the
first seven years of the NFL

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:22.540
career.

00:32:22.540 --> 00:32:25.300
And when I took those
initial right tackle sets

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:26.730
and I just felt so awkward.

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:29.220
My body was just so
programmed to move

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.440
from a left-handed stance.

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:35.700
I wanted to basically
quit in training camp

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:37.620
because I felt like I
was just so worthless

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:40.830
and that I was no longer
one of those five guys

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:42.360
I was accountable
to one another,

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:43.860
and that I couldn't
do my job, and I

00:32:43.860 --> 00:32:45.480
didn't want to do that to
the group and the team.

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:48.090
And it's never fun when you
have to learn through playing

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:52.235
in front of 60,000-plus
people, but, again,

00:32:52.235 --> 00:32:53.610
just kind of keep
your head down,

00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:56.400
and try to get better and
correct things and fix it

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:56.980
as you go.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:58.980
And that's what I did
and figured it out.

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:02.730
And so it's nice being able
to take some of those lessons

00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:06.820
and now try to apply them
outside of football, trying

00:33:06.820 --> 00:33:09.120
to raise a young
family and you know now

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:13.500
being back in West Michigan,
and just life changing sometimes

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:15.420
you feel like you can
maybe lose your footing.

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:19.230
But with what I've
learned, I'm able to stay

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:22.320
grounded, if that makes sense.

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.570
Something that everyone
who's gone to Hillsdale

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:28.200
has heard a million
times: it's the people.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:30.030
And I think it really
applies, because

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:32.220
without that great
group of people there,

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:35.550
I don't know how many people
would stick around and survive

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:38.117
and thrive and have a
really good academic career,

00:33:38.117 --> 00:33:39.450
athletic career, whatever it is.

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:41.050
It's a tough place.

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:43.000
So it was a losing
record my freshman year.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:46.470
And then it seemed like each
year, I think it was each year,

00:33:46.470 --> 00:33:49.165
we had gotten better,
improved, and then

00:33:49.165 --> 00:33:51.540
my senior year we had made it
to the Division II playoffs

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:53.082
for the first time
in school history.

00:33:53.082 --> 00:33:55.920
And Coach Otterbein
was a great coach.

00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:59.190
I really tip my hat to
him being able to work

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:04.990
with 18- to 22-year-old
kids and turn them into men.

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:07.960
And I think that was
always his number one goal.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:11.429
And when you're going through
the same kind of challenges,

00:34:11.429 --> 00:34:14.790
the same difficulties,
the same stages of life,

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:18.090
and you're doing it
together nonstop,

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:20.400
you just forge some really
special relationships.

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:22.864
And I was definitely able
to do that at Hillsdale.

00:34:22.864 --> 00:34:24.989
Not trying to put words in
your mouth, Coach Otter,

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:28.710
but if you can mold those good
men, those good human beings,

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:30.239
that the wins would follow that.

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:32.639
Because that was the
most important piece.

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:34.920
Without that then the
wins wouldn't happen.

00:34:38.912 --> 00:34:41.407
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:50.863 --> 00:34:51.780
KERRY CUMMINGS: Jared.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:52.988
Will you please come forward?

00:34:55.659 --> 00:34:56.560
And Dr. Arnn?

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:13.460
I'll have you stand here and
Dr. Arnn will be next to you.

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:15.770
Jared, on behalf of
the Alumni Association

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:17.810
of Hillsdale College,
it's my great honor

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:19.856
to present you with a--

00:35:19.856 --> 00:35:21.788
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:31.320
--Distinguished
Alumni Achievement

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:33.420
Award for Professional
Athletics.

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:34.260
Congratulations.

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:37.697
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:48.513 --> 00:35:49.930
LARRY ARNN: Just
right now I doubt

00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:51.800
the truth of the statement
the bigger they are,

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:52.720
the harder they fall.

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:54.193
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:56.847 --> 00:35:59.180
JARED VELDHEER: I don't think
any tall people said that.

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.005
No, where's Mr. Lantis?

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:12.140
What happened to the
revision on the--

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:14.990
I am no longer the
Strategies Wealth Advisors

00:36:14.990 --> 00:36:16.530
Health and Wellness Consultant.

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:18.530
I have now moved on to
bigger and better things.

00:36:18.530 --> 00:36:23.450
I am the school lunch lady for
St. Paul the Apostle Elementary

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:25.880
School in Grand Rapids.

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:28.160
I got the blisters to
prove it on my hands

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:30.260
from stirring up a
40-pound batch of Sloppy

00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:33.650
Joes over the last two days.

00:36:33.650 --> 00:36:36.740
Yeah, that's where
I am right now.

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:38.180
How I got there?

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:41.450
This place, this college
played a big part.

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:43.550
It taught me a lot
of great lessons that

00:36:43.550 --> 00:36:45.740
have helped mold
me into who I am

00:36:45.740 --> 00:36:50.450
into the friend, the father,
the spouse, the son, brother,

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:51.620
whatever it is in life.

00:36:51.620 --> 00:36:54.080
I've been able to take
away great lessons

00:36:54.080 --> 00:36:56.780
from this school
in the classroom,

00:36:56.780 --> 00:37:00.110
on the football field,
in the mule room, just

00:37:00.110 --> 00:37:02.000
random conversations.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:05.180
There's so much that you take in
when you're a part of Hillsdale

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:07.580
and Hillsdale College.

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:12.350
One of the biggest things is
perseverance and preparedness.

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:16.490
I think those are two
important pieces in life.

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:19.850
And with those you
can overcome anything

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:21.350
because you have a
plan and then you

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:24.650
can get through that
plan, whatever happens.

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:27.992
My most recent story with that,
really over the last two years,

00:37:27.992 --> 00:37:30.200
I planned on being done with
football, being retired,

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:32.810
but I've always been
kind of a fitness junkie,

00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:35.438
so I stayed in decent enough
shape and got to the point

00:37:35.438 --> 00:37:37.730
where I got kind of itchy
towards the end of the season

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:39.920
and asked my agent
if anybody needed

00:37:39.920 --> 00:37:43.820
help, and so I was
somewhat prepared.

00:37:43.820 --> 00:37:48.560
But the last go-round with
the Indianapolis Colts

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:50.250
was a little different.

00:37:50.250 --> 00:37:53.030
It was the day after Christmas,
it was a previous O-line coach,

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:55.100
and I got in the car,
they needed help.

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:58.910
I went down there since
Christmas Day was done.

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:02.220
I couldn't go back on Christmas
plans with the family,

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:04.040
it was our first
one back at home.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:07.107
So the GM actually reached
out earlier in December

00:38:07.107 --> 00:38:09.440
and I told him we'd have to
circle back after Christmas.

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:12.410
Well, day after Christmas I'm
in the car going down to Indy,

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:15.470
and I was just told they
needed help on the O-line

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:18.860
and I was willing to
pitch in and put my hand

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:20.900
in the pile, whatever it took.

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:23.150
But when I got down
there, they made

00:38:23.150 --> 00:38:25.640
it clear that they really
needed a line, and they're like,

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:30.320
do you think you can learn
this and start the next week?

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:33.260
And I was like, oh yeah
sure, yeah, let's do it.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:34.790
Why not?

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:36.835
And so I'm in the hotel.

00:38:36.835 --> 00:38:38.960
There's a five-day COVID
protocol where you're just

00:38:38.960 --> 00:38:40.640
sitting in a hotel
doing COVID testing

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:42.285
and you can't be with the team.

00:38:42.285 --> 00:38:44.160
You can do Zoom meetings,
that kind of stuff.

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:47.420
So my first day in the
building was a Thursday.

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:53.280
And to put it in some
perspective, you play Sunday.

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:56.030
And so I get there on
Thursday, meet all the players

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:58.430
for the first time in
person, actually have

00:38:58.430 --> 00:39:02.253
one legitimate practice, have
kind of a glorified walkthrough

00:39:02.253 --> 00:39:03.920
on Friday, true
walkthrough on Saturday,

00:39:03.920 --> 00:39:05.330
and start the game on Sunday.

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:08.773
And I kind of pinched myself
all the way through it,

00:39:08.773 --> 00:39:10.190
because I was like,
this is crazy.

00:39:10.190 --> 00:39:12.110
This is absolute craziness.

00:39:12.110 --> 00:39:14.210
This is the highest
level of football.

00:39:14.210 --> 00:39:16.730
And to the average
person this would just

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:20.990
seem like utter unpreparedness.

00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:23.420
There's no part of this
that should be happening.

00:39:23.420 --> 00:39:26.810
But luckily I was able
to have a background that

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:29.750
taught me how to prepare,
taught me how to take those five

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:31.490
days in the hotel
and transition it

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:34.970
into being able to play on
Sunday and lean on the lessons

00:39:34.970 --> 00:39:36.950
that I've learned the
10 years prior to that

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:41.390
and was able to step out there
and play in a game that Sunday.

00:39:41.390 --> 00:39:43.990
And then play in the next week
versus the Bills in a playoff

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:44.490
game.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.990
So just kind of a quick
synopsis on some of the things

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:49.640
that this college has taught me.

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:53.690
And then the perseverance
side is just not quitting,

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:54.290
not giving up.

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:58.790
Like I said in that video, and
it's hard to watch that and not

00:39:58.790 --> 00:40:00.350
get emotional with
it, because that

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:06.650
was tough to have to
relearn something completely

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:09.890
just because someone said,
that's what you're doing now.

00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:11.480
I really honestly
did want to quit.

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:13.010
I wanted to be done.

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:15.680
And again, that's where
I'm really grateful

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:20.720
for my family, my friends,
the support around me

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:24.500
to get through that and to
reinforce that I could do it,

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:26.990
you just got to give
it time, have a plan,

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:29.720
lean on that
preparedness, and flex

00:40:29.720 --> 00:40:31.040
those perseverance muscles.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:34.230
And it's like anything,
they build up over time.

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:38.300
So anyone here, if there's
one takeaway from tonight,

00:40:38.300 --> 00:40:40.400
just be prepared, have
a plan and persevere

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:41.150
through that plan.

00:40:41.150 --> 00:40:44.280
And this school definitely
taught me how to do that.

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:45.140
So thank you guys.

00:40:49.090 --> 00:40:50.020
Can't forget my award.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:51.760
And Mr Lantis,
could you make sure

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:55.630
that next time you make sure
the school lunch lady is

00:40:55.630 --> 00:40:58.870
on the official transcript?

00:40:58.870 --> 00:41:00.250
That's a proud moment in light.

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:03.400
Not too many lunch
ladies can get up here

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:05.545
and accept this award.

00:41:05.545 --> 00:41:17.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:19.850
KERRY CUMMINGS: Our next
honoree this evening

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:22.580
is Joy Pullmann,
who happens to be

00:41:22.580 --> 00:41:25.730
from the stellar class of 2009.

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:30.110
Joy manages to artfully balance
a career as managing editor

00:41:30.110 --> 00:41:34.340
of The Federalist, a web
magazine on politics, policy

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:39.260
and culture, with being a wife
and mother of six children.

00:41:39.260 --> 00:41:41.390
Joy also works as
a research fellow

00:41:41.390 --> 00:41:44.930
on education policy for
the Heartland Institute.

00:41:44.930 --> 00:41:47.870
As the former managing
editor of School Reform

00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:50.090
News Joy interviewed
and produced

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:51.710
podcasts with many
of the leading

00:41:51.710 --> 00:41:54.470
figures in school reform.

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:58.040
She was also assistant editor
for The American magazine

00:41:58.040 --> 00:42:00.560
at the American
Enterprise Institute.

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:05.630
Joy is the 2013-14 recipient
of Robert Novak Journalism

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:09.740
Fellowship for in-depth
reporting on Common Core

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:12.200
National Education standards.

00:42:12.200 --> 00:42:17.570
Joy's accomplished
journalism has

00:42:17.570 --> 00:42:21.140
been published by The New York
Times, Washington Examiner,

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:25.310
The Weekly Standard, Washington
Times, Milwaukee Journal

00:42:25.310 --> 00:42:30.010
Sentinel, Salt Lake Tribune,
National Review online,

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:34.300
Real Clear Policy, and
various other US newspapers

00:42:34.300 --> 00:42:35.920
and outlets.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:39.010
Joy has written a series of
research and commentary reports

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:43.060
on the Parent Tiger, a new
school reform idea sweeping

00:42:43.060 --> 00:42:47.440
the country, and is co-author
with Joseph L. Bast,

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:48.940
The Parent Trigger--

00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:51.070
Justification and
Design Guidelines,

00:42:51.070 --> 00:42:54.010
Heartland Institute, 2012.

00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:57.070
Joy has taught middle and
high school students history,

00:42:57.070 --> 00:43:00.760
literature and debate, and has
authored high school public

00:43:00.760 --> 00:43:02.080
speaking curriculum.

00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:05.570
She has traveled nationwide
to speak at prominent venues,

00:43:05.570 --> 00:43:06.700
including CPAC.

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:08.740
The National Right
to Life convention,

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:11.320
and statewide
education conferences.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:13.760
She has been guest on
numerous talk shows,

00:43:13.760 --> 00:43:16.930
including Fox and Friends,
The John Stossel Show,

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:19.600
and Wall Street Journal's
Opinion Journal.

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:21.940
Joy graduated from
Hillsdale with an English

00:43:21.940 --> 00:43:24.580
major and journalism
concentration.

00:43:24.580 --> 00:43:26.710
She lives in Indiana
with her husband,

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:30.850
Nathaniel, also a 2009 Hillsdale
graduate, and their six

00:43:30.850 --> 00:43:31.810
children.

00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:33.580
Please give your
attention to the screen

00:43:33.580 --> 00:43:36.595
to meet our Distinguished
Alumni honoree, Joy Pullmann.

00:43:38.602 --> 00:43:40.185
JOY PULLMANN: My
name is Joy Pullmann,

00:43:40.185 --> 00:43:43.890
and I'm a mother of six kids.

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:46.950
And I'm the executive
editor at The Federalist.

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:48.600
I get up at 5:30.

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:52.260
I may have 6,000 words to
edit it between 6:00 and 8:00

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:55.380
AM, may have 50 emails
to answer as well.

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:57.600
Then I head to the kitchen
to see what state it's

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:00.333
in with all of my children
hanging from the chandeliers--

00:44:00.333 --> 00:44:01.500
we don't have a chandeliers.

00:44:01.500 --> 00:44:05.180
1:30-2 o'clock is nap
time, and then I'm working.

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:09.780
I get a lot from young
women, how do you do it?

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:12.400
I say, try not to
if you can help it.

00:44:17.340 --> 00:44:19.473
Looking at all the college
options that I had,

00:44:19.473 --> 00:44:21.390
why was I going to college
in the first place?

00:44:21.390 --> 00:44:22.973
I was going to college
to be educated,

00:44:22.973 --> 00:44:25.650
to take the talents that I
had and make the most of it.

00:44:25.650 --> 00:44:28.020
I had a gut feeling that
at Hillsdale is where

00:44:28.020 --> 00:44:29.730
I would get the best education.

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:32.670
The only thing I wish that
would have been better

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:35.400
about my Hillsdale
experience was me.

00:44:35.400 --> 00:44:38.732
Well, you know you're
18 years old and stupid.

00:44:38.732 --> 00:44:41.190
I was an English major, and I
knew that from the beginning.

00:44:41.190 --> 00:44:43.590
I love to write.

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:45.570
What I loved really about
reporting in college

00:44:45.570 --> 00:44:47.970
was being a conduit for
other people's stories.

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:49.947
How do you get
people interested?

00:44:49.947 --> 00:44:51.780
How do you make sure
it's organized clearly?

00:44:51.780 --> 00:44:53.670
All of those kind of
bits about the craft.

00:44:53.670 --> 00:44:56.460
After Hillsdale,
I did huge amounts

00:44:56.460 --> 00:44:57.780
of on-the-ground reporting.

00:44:57.780 --> 00:44:59.910
I was one of the earliest
people to even pay

00:44:59.910 --> 00:45:01.920
any attention to this
thing called Common Core.

00:45:04.630 --> 00:45:07.010
It came to me from the
grassroots from a mom.

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:10.403
She called me one day and said,
why aren't there any reporters

00:45:10.403 --> 00:45:12.070
covering this thing
called Common Core I

00:45:12.070 --> 00:45:14.290
think this was 2012 or 2013.

00:45:14.290 --> 00:45:17.680
It was before this became
a huge grassroots issue.

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:20.530
She was saying, my local school
district is talking about it

00:45:20.530 --> 00:45:22.570
and I tried to go to a
public meeting about it

00:45:22.570 --> 00:45:23.890
and they wouldn't let me in.

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:28.630
I was instantly, on her behalf,
wondering, OK, this is weird.

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:29.620
This shouldn't happen.

00:45:29.620 --> 00:45:32.020
Government 101, the
people are allowed

00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:33.820
to go to public meetings.

00:45:33.820 --> 00:45:35.715
I also tried to get
into that meeting.

00:45:35.715 --> 00:45:37.090
They wouldn't talk
to me about it

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:38.350
or let me know
anything about it.

00:45:38.350 --> 00:45:40.000
I just published a
story about the fact

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:43.060
that they weren't letting
anyone do that information.

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:46.690
And I got deluged with
emails from people saying,

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:47.980
we heard about this, too.

00:45:47.980 --> 00:45:49.930
What is this thing?

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:53.110
I happened to be someone just
doing basic journalism when

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:56.020
no other outlets were doing
basic journalism on this thing

00:45:56.020 --> 00:45:58.480
that really aimed to completely
overhaul and nationalize

00:45:58.480 --> 00:45:59.590
American education.

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:02.800
Because of that, I got a
huge following, huge amount

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:06.540
of readership, and that
turned into a book.

00:46:06.540 --> 00:46:09.360
Then it started getting
huge media attention

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:12.735
and became a thing that
even eventually Donald Trump

00:46:12.735 --> 00:46:14.190
started talking about.

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:19.800
Reporting on Common Core
played into our decision

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.990
to start a school.

00:46:21.990 --> 00:46:24.690
People need to be more
and better educated.

00:46:24.690 --> 00:46:27.360
The education is a
formation of a human soul,

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:30.690
and the kind of souls that
you have in your country

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:32.940
determine whether it
can be self-governing.

00:46:32.940 --> 00:46:34.920
And I want my children
to be self-governing

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:37.410
and I want their neighbors
to be self-governing.

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:40.890
So we started Redeemer
Classical School in 2016.

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:43.910
And it was one-room
schoolhouse then.

00:46:43.910 --> 00:46:45.930
Now we're a four-room
schoolhouse.

00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:48.420
My husband is the
headmaster of the school

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:52.560
and I'm the executive
editor at The Federalist.

00:46:52.560 --> 00:46:54.930
When you have a heart
commitment to the things that

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:57.670
are good, for me it's
really got me through a lot.

00:46:57.670 --> 00:47:01.590
I mean, doing all
the things that I do

00:47:01.590 --> 00:47:03.850
has been basically a
battle the whole time.

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:07.830
So you don't have the heart
to fight without someone

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:10.990
having shown you what's
worth fighting for.

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:15.200
And so that's what I really got
from the classes at Hillsdale.

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:18.990
Taught me what to love, rather
than fighting and bitterness.

00:47:18.990 --> 00:47:22.410
Dr. Sundahl was one of my
freshman English professors

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:24.870
and then I took him, of
course, a bunch of other times.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:26.730
The things that he would
talk about in class

00:47:26.730 --> 00:47:29.430
were just so
touching that I would

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:32.370
sit in the back of his class
and cry as quietly as I could.

00:47:32.370 --> 00:47:34.440
I think of Hillsdale as
kind of like the shining

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:35.310
city on a hill.

00:47:35.310 --> 00:47:39.058
It's this beautiful place that
is so unlike almost everywhere

00:47:39.058 --> 00:47:40.350
else that we have in the world.

00:47:40.350 --> 00:47:42.270
And of course it's
not a perfect place,

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:45.750
but it has so many excellencies
all together in one place.

00:47:45.750 --> 00:47:47.970
And people have
worked over centuries

00:47:47.970 --> 00:47:50.980
and they sacrificed
to make that possible.

00:47:50.980 --> 00:47:52.620
So you can go there
and get refreshed,

00:47:52.620 --> 00:47:56.260
you can go there and have hope.

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:58.450
Our country is in
really dangerous times.

00:47:58.450 --> 00:48:01.960
Through my work, you see some of
that grave danger in Hillsdale

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:04.840
is for me what gives
me so often the courage

00:48:04.840 --> 00:48:08.495
to get out there and
keep taking swings.

00:48:08.495 --> 00:48:10.120
Right when I graduated,
I really wished

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:12.264
I could go back and
do it all over again.

00:48:12.264 --> 00:48:15.722
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:36.970 --> 00:48:41.680
KERRY CUMMINGS: OK Joy, on
behalf of Alumni Association

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:44.020
of Hillsdale College,
it's my great honor

00:48:44.020 --> 00:48:46.360
to present you with the
Distinguished Alumni

00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:48.130
Award in Journalism.

00:48:48.130 --> 00:48:48.910
Congratulations.

00:48:48.910 --> 00:48:52.234
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:04.960 --> 00:49:07.120
JOY PULLMAN: Well,
Hillsdale made me a writer

00:49:07.120 --> 00:49:09.400
and my husband a
teacher, so they are not

00:49:09.400 --> 00:49:14.110
giving me this award for all the
big checks that we write them.

00:49:14.110 --> 00:49:16.540
All the big checks that we
have we put into the school

00:49:16.540 --> 00:49:18.820
pretty much.

00:49:18.820 --> 00:49:22.340
But as I was thinking about
talking to you all tonight,

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:24.130
I did think about
some checks that

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:27.640
really mattered a lot to me
that have come to Hillsdale.

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:30.490
They came by the hand
of my father, who

00:49:30.490 --> 00:49:34.840
wrote his portion of that
6% for my college tuition.

00:49:34.840 --> 00:49:37.360
He was unable to
be here tonight,

00:49:37.360 --> 00:49:39.760
but I know that he's
watching so I wanted

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:45.130
to recognize the contributions
that my father made and made

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:47.810
it possible for me to go to
Hillsdale in the first place.

00:49:47.810 --> 00:49:50.710
Now I know what my father did
to earn that tuition money.

00:49:50.710 --> 00:49:53.890
I'm the daughter of a
farmer, so when I was a child

00:49:53.890 --> 00:49:56.860
I would see him come home.

00:49:56.860 --> 00:49:59.170
Sometimes I would hear from
my mother he'd come home

00:49:59.170 --> 00:50:00.910
at midnight, gone
back out 3:00 AM

00:50:00.910 --> 00:50:03.040
to check on the
irrigation pumps,

00:50:03.040 --> 00:50:05.620
and I would see him
sometimes come back

00:50:05.620 --> 00:50:08.320
from what they call the grain
bins covered in white dust,

00:50:08.320 --> 00:50:10.480
smelling really, really awful.

00:50:10.480 --> 00:50:15.760
And when I came to
school here and I

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:18.820
knew that what my father
had done to make it possible

00:50:18.820 --> 00:50:20.770
for me to be here,
that is why I didn't

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:23.380
spend a lot of time partying.

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:26.260
So although perhaps
those checks were

00:50:26.260 --> 00:50:29.500
small in the scheme of things,
they matter a lot to me

00:50:29.500 --> 00:50:32.800
and I want to thank all of
you for your 6% or maybe more

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:35.510
that you have given
to make it possible.

00:50:35.510 --> 00:50:38.650
And I want to say especially
thanks, Dad, for what you did.

00:50:38.650 --> 00:50:40.900
Because without you
having done that,

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:42.380
I wouldn't be here right now.

00:50:42.380 --> 00:50:43.895
So this is really for you Dad.

00:50:43.895 --> 00:50:44.395
Thanks.

00:50:44.395 --> 00:50:46.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:56.350
I also wanted to
recognize people who are

00:50:56.350 --> 00:50:58.060
as important to me as family.

00:50:58.060 --> 00:51:00.670
So another one of the
most glorious things

00:51:00.670 --> 00:51:03.460
about my Hillsdale
experience was the people,

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:06.790
was the friendships that
we made here on campus.

00:51:06.790 --> 00:51:11.170
And even though you
heard about my bio

00:51:11.170 --> 00:51:14.230
and it sounds probably really
great and people kind of

00:51:14.230 --> 00:51:17.710
sauce those out to
make them look better.

00:51:17.710 --> 00:51:20.950
So I've flown to New York
City to go on Fox News.

00:51:20.950 --> 00:51:24.520
But you know what really is
much, much more important

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:27.257
than any of the things on my
bio is seeing the faces that

00:51:27.257 --> 00:51:28.465
are right here at this table.

00:51:32.150 --> 00:51:34.630
I'm looking at each one of
these people who most of whom we

00:51:34.630 --> 00:51:37.450
went to college same class.

00:51:37.450 --> 00:51:38.410
Well, maybe I won't.

00:51:38.410 --> 00:51:41.230
I was about to say personal
things about all of you.

00:51:41.230 --> 00:51:43.580
You know who you are.

00:51:43.580 --> 00:51:46.780
People at this table
have written checks

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:49.240
for our school to
start even if it

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:53.260
wasn't possible for their
kids to go to the school.

00:51:53.260 --> 00:51:55.780
People at this
table have shown up

00:51:55.780 --> 00:52:01.990
when I was flat on my back sick,
picked up my children for me.

00:52:01.990 --> 00:52:07.210
They got an email from me saying
I'm going to get this award,

00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:11.110
I can't believe it, you want
to come have some free wine?

00:52:11.110 --> 00:52:12.653
Just kidding.

00:52:12.653 --> 00:52:15.070
We're not that low anymore,
we just used to be in college.

00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:21.130
So I mean, there's
nothing that's

00:52:21.130 --> 00:52:25.420
more important than the
people at that table.

00:52:25.420 --> 00:52:28.690
And I would rather have their
honor and their presence

00:52:28.690 --> 00:52:32.630
and their esteem far more than
pretty much anything else.

00:52:32.630 --> 00:52:35.410
So that's who I'm here for
tonight and I thank you

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:36.580
all for coming.

00:52:36.580 --> 00:52:39.540
And thank you to Dr. Arnn and
for the college for this honor.

00:52:39.540 --> 00:52:41.470
I don't know that I
deserve it, but I'll

00:52:41.470 --> 00:52:44.350
do my best to be worth--

00:52:44.350 --> 00:52:47.990
to use your name well.

00:52:47.990 --> 00:52:48.730
So thank you all.

00:52:48.730 --> 00:52:52.586
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:01.760 --> 00:53:03.610
KERRY CUMMINGS: Thank you, Joy.

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:06.160
Receiving our third
Distinguished Alumnus Award

00:53:06.160 --> 00:53:10.540
this evening is Howard
Smith, class of 1968.

00:53:10.540 --> 00:53:12.800
Howard was born in Dayton, Ohio.

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:14.980
He earned a Bachelor
of Arts in history

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:17.260
with a minor in
business from Hillsdale.

00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:20.050
He was involved in a number
of campus activities,

00:53:20.050 --> 00:53:22.840
including Delta Tau
Delta fraternity,

00:53:22.840 --> 00:53:26.620
and was a member of both the
football and track teams.

00:53:26.620 --> 00:53:28.180
During his freshman year.

00:53:28.180 --> 00:53:31.510
Howard met Pamela
Norris, class of '68.

00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:34.120
They married in 1971.

00:53:34.120 --> 00:53:39.520
Pamela passed away in 2013
after 42 years of marriage.

00:53:39.520 --> 00:53:42.730
After graduation, Smith
spent the next four years

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:46.180
serving his country in the
United States Coast Guard.

00:53:46.180 --> 00:53:49.390
In 1973, he went to
work for Pam's family

00:53:49.390 --> 00:53:53.440
business, the Wilson
Bohannon Lock Company.

00:53:53.440 --> 00:53:57.160
Howard spent the first 22
years of his 48-year career

00:53:57.160 --> 00:54:00.520
at Wilson Bohannon in the
accounting department.

00:54:00.520 --> 00:54:04.750
In 1995 he became
president and CEO.

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:09.370
During this time he earned an
MBA from Ashland University.

00:54:09.370 --> 00:54:13.690
The company is proud to be a
100% American-made manufacturer

00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:16.420
of brass padlocks
and locking devices

00:54:16.420 --> 00:54:19.180
that can be found
all over the world.

00:54:19.180 --> 00:54:23.440
Howard and Pam's two
daughters, Sarah Smith Wrastle

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:27.490
'08 and Trish Smith, as well as
the son-in-law Aaron Wrastle,

00:54:27.490 --> 00:54:31.120
are the seventh generation
to work for the company.

00:54:31.120 --> 00:54:34.630
Making a difference in his
local community of Marion, Ohio,

00:54:34.630 --> 00:54:37.780
has always been a high
priority for Howard.

00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:39.830
He has served on
numerous boards,

00:54:39.830 --> 00:54:44.170
including the Convention and
Visitors Bureau, the Red Cross,

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:45.580
and the YMCA.

00:54:45.580 --> 00:54:48.580
He's the past president
of the Marion Rotary Club,

00:54:48.580 --> 00:54:53.680
Marion Can-Do Development board,
and the YMCA endowment board.

00:54:53.680 --> 00:54:57.070
He currently serves on the
Marion Can-Do and Marion

00:54:57.070 --> 00:54:58.720
General Hospital boards.

00:54:58.720 --> 00:55:01.060
He's a member of the
Manufacturers Council,

00:55:01.060 --> 00:55:05.110
the Industrial Club, and Rotary.

00:55:05.110 --> 00:55:07.720
Smith and the Wilson
Bohannon Lock Company

00:55:07.720 --> 00:55:09.490
have been honored
in recent years.

00:55:09.490 --> 00:55:12.850
In 2018, he was inducted
into the Lock Collectors

00:55:12.850 --> 00:55:17.350
Hall of Fame, and in 2019
received a Lifetime Achievement

00:55:17.350 --> 00:55:19.930
Award from the Conway
Family Business Center

00:55:19.930 --> 00:55:21.670
in Columbus, Ohio.

00:55:21.670 --> 00:55:25.270
In 2020, the Wilson
Bohannon Lock Company

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:28.870
was chosen to represent Ohio
at the fourth annual Made

00:55:28.870 --> 00:55:32.110
in America product
showcase at the White House

00:55:32.110 --> 00:55:33.790
with President Trump.

00:55:33.790 --> 00:55:35.560
Please give your
attention to the screen

00:55:35.560 --> 00:55:38.455
to our distinguished alumnus
honoree, Howard Smith.

00:55:46.850 --> 00:55:51.170
HOWARD SMITH: First you listen,
and you learn, and you watch,

00:55:51.170 --> 00:55:53.160
and you wait your opportunity.

00:55:53.160 --> 00:55:55.850
And then when you're given
opportunity you do the best

00:55:55.850 --> 00:55:57.740
you possibly can with
that opportunity.

00:55:57.740 --> 00:56:01.320
It's easy to be successful
when all the times are good.

00:56:01.320 --> 00:56:03.465
It's when the times
are not good and how

00:56:03.465 --> 00:56:05.840
do you bridge that to where
the time started getting good

00:56:05.840 --> 00:56:06.340
again?

00:56:06.340 --> 00:56:08.810
And I believe if
you apply yourself

00:56:08.810 --> 00:56:10.925
the American dream is
possible for anybody today.

00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:16.232
My name is Howard Smith.

00:56:16.232 --> 00:56:17.940
I'm president of the
Wilson Bohannon Lock

00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:19.920
Company, Marion, Ohio.

00:56:19.920 --> 00:56:23.550
My wife's grandfather,
Mr. Wilson Bohannon Tway

00:56:23.550 --> 00:56:25.470
came through here
on a troop train,

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:29.100
stopped at a canteen stop and
remembered the friendliness

00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:33.420
of the people in Marion, so
here is where we came in 1926

00:56:33.420 --> 00:56:36.300
and here is where we are
today, almost 100 years later

00:56:36.300 --> 00:56:38.700
with about 70 employees,
making padlocks that

00:56:38.700 --> 00:56:40.753
are used all over the world.

00:56:40.753 --> 00:56:42.420
We're a seven-generation
family business

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:45.000
and very proud to be
100% American made.

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:49.230
Went to high school in
Dayton, Ohio, and about 95%

00:56:49.230 --> 00:56:51.730
of my high school class
goes on to college.

00:56:51.730 --> 00:56:55.020
So my parents are going crazy at
the end of the whole situation.

00:56:55.020 --> 00:56:57.870
All my friends are
enrolled somewhere,

00:56:57.870 --> 00:56:59.380
and where's my son going to go?

00:56:59.380 --> 00:57:02.040
But you know Hillsdale,
with the conservative values

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:03.900
and the student guide,
it just rang a bell.

00:57:03.900 --> 00:57:04.650
This is beautiful.

00:57:04.650 --> 00:57:07.350
And they said, this is
where we want you to go.

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:09.360
I met my wife my
third week at school.

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:11.820
Her great-great grandfather
started the company

00:57:11.820 --> 00:57:14.760
in Brooklyn, New York in
1860 with his son Todd

00:57:14.760 --> 00:57:16.920
in his shed behind their house.

00:57:16.920 --> 00:57:19.770
She didn't want to
be in the business,

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:22.000
she wanted to be
part of the business,

00:57:22.000 --> 00:57:24.210
and so she was on the
board of directors.

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:25.920
She was a guidance
counselor Mary

00:57:25.920 --> 00:57:28.510
Catholic for over 30 years.

00:57:28.510 --> 00:57:31.320
She loved her school,
she loved her community,

00:57:31.320 --> 00:57:34.920
she loved this company,
and people just loved Pam.

00:57:34.920 --> 00:57:38.730
She was kind, loving,
just a big heart.

00:57:38.730 --> 00:57:41.610
There's always a place for any
student to come to the panel

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:44.340
and talk to her
about any situation.

00:57:44.340 --> 00:57:46.450
And her passion for
Wilson Bohannon company

00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:48.690
was powerful and infectious.

00:57:48.690 --> 00:57:50.610
Everybody knew how much
she love this place

00:57:50.610 --> 00:57:53.670
and we built that legacy
with my two daughters.

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:55.440
And sadly she was
she passed away

00:57:55.440 --> 00:57:57.060
after 42 years of marriage.

00:57:57.060 --> 00:57:59.070
What better way could
you carry on her memory

00:57:59.070 --> 00:58:02.910
and it be something she had so
much pride in, this company?

00:58:02.910 --> 00:58:06.550
We make over 5,000 locks a
day, with most [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:06.550 --> 00:58:09.630
all made to order to the exact
specification of our customers.

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:12.600
There's no lock manufacturer
like this in America today

00:58:12.600 --> 00:58:15.400
that can do the things we can
do with the tolerance we can do.

00:58:15.400 --> 00:58:17.370
So I'm proud of that,
proud that we're

00:58:17.370 --> 00:58:20.790
made in this country, proud
that the symbol like Hillsdale

00:58:20.790 --> 00:58:23.688
talks about, the
conservative values

00:58:23.688 --> 00:58:25.230
and their Christian
attitude-- that's

00:58:25.230 --> 00:58:27.563
the way we run our business,
with a Christian attitude--

00:58:27.563 --> 00:58:29.970
and the belief in
the Constitution.

00:58:29.970 --> 00:58:33.790
But Hillsdale taught me to
be a good corporate citizen.

00:58:33.790 --> 00:58:36.318
I want to give back I want a
place where my daughters can

00:58:36.318 --> 00:58:37.110
raise their family.

00:58:37.110 --> 00:58:40.560
I want a place where my
employees can feel good

00:58:40.560 --> 00:58:41.295
about themselves.

00:58:41.295 --> 00:58:43.170
And when you hire people
and you bring people

00:58:43.170 --> 00:58:45.370
from other parts of
the country, are you

00:58:45.370 --> 00:58:47.745
going to bring them to someplace
they don't want to live?

00:58:53.370 --> 00:58:55.530
Hillsdale just is
that signpost, that

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:59.230
guidepost telling us how is
the best way to do things.

00:58:59.230 --> 00:59:01.890
I think I said before
the American dream is

00:59:01.890 --> 00:59:03.820
alive and well in this country.

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:06.270
And I just want
to be an example.

00:59:06.270 --> 00:59:09.360
We're not just a business, we're
a human business with human

00:59:09.360 --> 00:59:13.350
people who care, with respect
and dignity of each other

00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:15.700
and building a culture that's--

00:59:15.700 --> 00:59:16.602
it's powerful.

00:59:16.602 --> 00:59:18.060
People who walk
through our factory

00:59:18.060 --> 00:59:21.540
say, gee whiz, everybody
has a good attitude,

00:59:21.540 --> 00:59:24.390
they like what they do,
they're proud of this company.

00:59:24.390 --> 00:59:26.730
Well, it's creating
a corporate culture

00:59:26.730 --> 00:59:30.360
that allows people to grow
and be something and be

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:34.140
part of a larger part of
something than just themselves.

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:35.190
It's not just me.

00:59:35.190 --> 00:59:36.870
It's all the people
that surround me,

00:59:36.870 --> 00:59:39.718
all the people that have
made this a great company.

00:59:39.718 --> 00:59:41.260
I couldn't do without
all the people.

00:59:41.260 --> 00:59:42.160
And that's what I do.

00:59:42.160 --> 00:59:43.868
And I go to the factory
every single day,

00:59:43.868 --> 00:59:46.514
I thank our employees,
thank you very much.

00:59:46.514 --> 00:59:47.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:59:48.490 --> 00:59:52.442
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:21.185 --> 01:00:22.805
Thank you so very much.

01:00:22.805 --> 01:00:24.180
KERRY CUMMINGS:
Howard, on behalf

01:00:24.180 --> 01:00:26.820
of the Alumni Association
of Hillsdale College

01:00:26.820 --> 01:00:29.610
it's my great honor to present
you with the Distinguished

01:00:29.610 --> 01:00:32.280
Alumnus Award for
Outstanding Professional

01:00:32.280 --> 01:00:34.050
Achievement in Management.

01:00:34.050 --> 01:00:34.770
Congratulations.

01:00:34.770 --> 01:00:38.574
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:55.330 --> 01:00:57.880
HOWARD SMITH: I want to thank
Hillsdale College and everyone

01:00:57.880 --> 01:01:01.510
who was involved in this award
ceremony tonight, especially

01:01:01.510 --> 01:01:06.580
Dr. Arnn, for this evening
they made possible.

01:01:06.580 --> 01:01:10.900
I want to thank my family and
friends who are here tonight.

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:15.520
I want to thank Penny and
Steve Miller from Columbus.

01:01:15.520 --> 01:01:18.820
Penny is a member of the
Pi Beta Phi sorority.

01:01:18.820 --> 01:01:22.920
And to Gerry Anne Rocco
from Dayton for all

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:24.730
he love and support.

01:01:24.730 --> 01:01:28.440
My daughter Sarah,
who was a student here

01:01:28.440 --> 01:01:31.260
and a member of Kappa Kappa
Gamma sorority, and her husband

01:01:31.260 --> 01:01:35.010
Aaron, who both work
for our family business.

01:01:35.010 --> 01:01:39.120
And to Doug Zimmerman,
from Boise, Idaho, who

01:01:39.120 --> 01:01:42.630
was a classmate, an outstanding
cross-country runner,

01:01:42.630 --> 01:01:45.690
and a member of the track team.

01:01:45.690 --> 01:01:49.010
And lastly Alan and
Beth Shafer from Dayton.

01:01:49.010 --> 01:01:51.950
Beth and my wife Pam were
sorority sisters of Chi Omega

01:01:51.950 --> 01:01:53.960
sorority.

01:01:53.960 --> 01:01:57.470
And Alan and I have
been lifelong friends

01:01:57.470 --> 01:02:01.040
Alan was a 1968 class
president and president

01:02:01.040 --> 01:02:03.650
of our fraternity
Delta Tau Delta.

01:02:03.650 --> 01:02:06.230
I look back on my
life and I ask myself

01:02:06.230 --> 01:02:11.480
what the Hillsdale College mean
to me as a student in the '60s?

01:02:11.480 --> 01:02:15.040
I wanted to make my
parents proud of me.

01:02:15.040 --> 01:02:16.450
I wanted to enjoy
the experience.

01:02:16.450 --> 01:02:18.742
I wanted to participate in
as many things as I possibly

01:02:18.742 --> 01:02:20.770
could, many activities.

01:02:20.770 --> 01:02:22.540
I was a history,
major and a business

01:02:22.540 --> 01:02:26.530
minor, student guide
for four years here,

01:02:26.530 --> 01:02:28.880
played football and
track and enjoyed--

01:02:28.880 --> 01:02:31.210
And I had a chance to
throw the javelin--

01:02:31.210 --> 01:02:33.550
something I couldn't do
obviously in high school.

01:02:33.550 --> 01:02:35.230
And a member of
the Delta Tau Delta

01:02:35.230 --> 01:02:38.980
fraternity, on the leadership
workshop and the union policy

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:40.330
board.

01:02:40.330 --> 01:02:43.990
In 2020, our
company was selected

01:02:43.990 --> 01:02:46.720
to be the fourth annual Made
in America product showcase

01:02:46.720 --> 01:02:48.580
to represent the state of Ohio.

01:02:48.580 --> 01:02:50.940
I thought, how could
any award I ever receive

01:02:50.940 --> 01:02:55.990
be any greater than this until
I got a call several months ago

01:02:55.990 --> 01:02:59.632
and I was told that I was going
to be recipient of this award.

01:02:59.632 --> 01:03:01.840
I said this is the most
outstanding thing I've ever--

01:03:01.840 --> 01:03:03.757
because I believe so
much in Hillsdale College

01:03:03.757 --> 01:03:05.650
and what it means today.

01:03:05.650 --> 01:03:07.990
Hillsdale gave me a
foundation for being

01:03:07.990 --> 01:03:10.060
a successful businessperson.

01:03:10.060 --> 01:03:13.055
After graduating from college,
I spent the next four years

01:03:13.055 --> 01:03:15.340
in the US Coast Guard.

01:03:15.340 --> 01:03:18.620
And I was married
in 1971 and 1973

01:03:18.620 --> 01:03:21.070
I started working for the
Wilson Bohannon Lock Company,

01:03:21.070 --> 01:03:25.150
first as an accountant
and then as its president.

01:03:25.150 --> 01:03:27.220
Wilson Bohannon looks
a lot different today

01:03:27.220 --> 01:03:29.410
than it did back
when I started there

01:03:29.410 --> 01:03:32.780
in 1973, almost 50 years ago.

01:03:32.780 --> 01:03:34.663
Back then, all we did
was that we assembled.

01:03:34.663 --> 01:03:37.330
Today, we're totally integrated,
making all of our manufacturing

01:03:37.330 --> 01:03:38.650
parts in one location.

01:03:41.910 --> 01:03:44.460
The two most powerful
words I believe in business

01:03:44.460 --> 01:03:47.220
today are
communication and love.

01:03:50.350 --> 01:03:52.780
I keep every one of our
officers and our staff

01:03:52.780 --> 01:03:56.530
included in our plans and our
visions in leading the company.

01:03:56.530 --> 01:03:58.690
Showing continuous
love and respect

01:03:58.690 --> 01:04:01.890
for all of our
associates is essential.

01:04:01.890 --> 01:04:04.350
The top five officers of
Wilson Bohannon Company

01:04:04.350 --> 01:04:08.100
have over 180 years'
experience in the lock industry

01:04:08.100 --> 01:04:09.690
or our company.

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:11.790
Transitioning from
the sixth generation

01:04:11.790 --> 01:04:14.130
to the seventh generation,
my two daughters

01:04:14.130 --> 01:04:16.680
and my son-in-law,
I hope someday

01:04:16.680 --> 01:04:19.660
that my grandchildren,
or the eighth generation,

01:04:19.660 --> 01:04:22.353
will continue on our legacy.

01:04:22.353 --> 01:04:24.270
Thank you, Hillsdale
College, for providing me

01:04:24.270 --> 01:04:29.040
with these tools I needed to
lead a seven-generation family

01:04:29.040 --> 01:04:33.480
business that is proud
to be 100% American made.

01:04:33.480 --> 01:04:35.460
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:41.900 --> 01:04:43.850
God bless our country.

01:04:43.850 --> 01:04:48.502
God bless Hillsdale College as
a defender of our Constitution.

01:04:48.502 --> 01:04:50.210
And thank you for this
great honor you've

01:04:50.210 --> 01:04:51.260
bestowed upon me tonight.

01:04:51.260 --> 01:04:54.025
[APPLAUSE]

01:05:11.490 --> 01:05:13.310
KERRY CUMMINGS:
Thank you, Howard.

01:05:13.310 --> 01:05:16.250
Receiving our fourth
distinguished award

01:05:16.250 --> 01:05:20.690
this evening is Ron
Gladnick, class of 1983.

01:05:20.690 --> 01:05:23.480
Ron is the head football
coach at Torrey Pines High

01:05:23.480 --> 01:05:27.710
School in San Diego,
California, and former CEO

01:05:27.710 --> 01:05:30.860
of Apical Industries,
which manufactures

01:05:30.860 --> 01:05:34.250
helicopter flotation systems.

01:05:34.250 --> 01:05:35.840
Pretty amazing.

01:05:35.840 --> 01:05:37.790
A defensive lineman
for the Chargers,

01:05:37.790 --> 01:05:43.010
Ron played on the 1980 and
1982 GLEAC championship teams

01:05:43.010 --> 01:05:44.840
and was named All-American.

01:05:44.840 --> 01:05:47.690
He was inducted into the
Hillsdale College Athletic

01:05:47.690 --> 01:05:50.180
Hall of Fame in 2008.

01:05:50.180 --> 01:05:52.670
Ron coached defensive
line for the Chargers

01:05:52.670 --> 01:05:55.100
and worked in the college's
advancement office

01:05:55.100 --> 01:05:59.030
before moving to
San Diego in 1991.

01:05:59.030 --> 01:06:02.150
There he established
Apical Industries,

01:06:02.150 --> 01:06:06.470
which grew to more than 500
employees in seven facilities,

01:06:06.470 --> 01:06:09.830
producing 90% of the market
share of helicopter flotation

01:06:09.830 --> 01:06:10.880
systems.

01:06:10.880 --> 01:06:16.520
In 2007 Dart Helicopter Services
acquired the business and Ron

01:06:16.520 --> 01:06:19.430
sold his interest in
the company in 2011

01:06:19.430 --> 01:06:23.330
but continues to work as a
partner in two aviation parts

01:06:23.330 --> 01:06:24.500
companies.

01:06:24.500 --> 01:06:28.130
Since 2014, Ron has served
as head football coach

01:06:28.130 --> 01:06:29.780
at Torrey Pines High School.

01:06:29.780 --> 01:06:32.750
He works tirelessly
with young men,

01:06:32.750 --> 01:06:36.350
turning a failing program
into a championship team.

01:06:36.350 --> 01:06:40.970
In 2021, Ron led a successful
grassroots campaign

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:43.190
to resume youth
sports competition

01:06:43.190 --> 01:06:47.330
in California, which had been
suspended due to COVID-19.

01:06:47.330 --> 01:06:50.480
Ron lives in a California beach
community just north of San

01:06:50.480 --> 01:06:52.280
Diego with his wife Joanna.

01:06:52.280 --> 01:06:56.178
He has two daughters, Karina
and Kayla, and at this point,

01:06:56.178 --> 01:06:58.220
I'd like you to turn your
attention to the screen

01:06:58.220 --> 01:06:58.970
to meet Ron.

01:07:01.845 --> 01:07:03.220
RON GLADNICK: And
the first thing

01:07:03.220 --> 01:07:06.768
we teach our ninth graders has
nothing to do with football.

01:07:06.768 --> 01:07:07.810
All right, hey, heads up.

01:07:10.840 --> 01:07:13.270
Summer's over.

01:07:13.270 --> 01:07:14.740
You know it's time
to go to work.

01:07:14.740 --> 01:07:16.865
Has everything to do with
how to shake a man's hand

01:07:16.865 --> 01:07:18.250
and how to look you in the eye.

01:07:18.250 --> 01:07:20.750
And that's where we start.

01:07:20.750 --> 01:07:23.780
It has everything to do with
building great young men.

01:07:23.780 --> 01:07:25.910
And I believe if you do
that the football will

01:07:25.910 --> 01:07:28.410
take care of itself.

01:07:28.410 --> 01:07:30.910
Hi, my name is Ron Gladnick,
and I'm the head football coach

01:07:30.910 --> 01:07:33.490
at Torrey Pines High School.

01:07:33.490 --> 01:07:36.490
At Hillsdale as a
freshman, I had the benefit

01:07:36.490 --> 01:07:39.700
of probably playing on
one of the worst football

01:07:39.700 --> 01:07:42.340
teams in the history
of Hillsdale College.

01:07:42.340 --> 01:07:44.260
We were 3 and 8.

01:07:44.260 --> 01:07:46.300
When Coach Dye and
Coach Lowry came

01:07:46.300 --> 01:07:48.070
to Hillsdale my
sophomore year we

01:07:48.070 --> 01:07:51.502
spent the next three months
at 6 o'clock in the morning

01:07:51.502 --> 01:07:53.710
where they had turned the
heat up in the field house,

01:07:53.710 --> 01:07:58.450
felt like 110, the
most brutal workouts,

01:07:58.450 --> 01:08:01.070
quite frankly to get guys who
weren't committed to leave,

01:08:01.070 --> 01:08:02.480
which a bunch of them did.

01:08:02.480 --> 01:08:06.070
And then over the
next three years

01:08:06.070 --> 01:08:11.140
I was a part of that transition
from being a very bad football

01:08:11.140 --> 01:08:14.320
team to being one of the
best Division II football

01:08:14.320 --> 01:08:15.889
teams in the country.

01:08:15.889 --> 01:08:18.760
I was a business major
and an English minor.

01:08:18.760 --> 01:08:21.170
I love a liberal arts
education for that reason.

01:08:21.170 --> 01:08:22.600
It creates well-rounded people.

01:08:22.600 --> 01:08:25.660
And like I tell everybody
the most valuable thing

01:08:25.660 --> 01:08:28.760
about Hillsdale College
is you learn how to learn.

01:08:28.760 --> 01:08:30.670
And as a result of
that, I've always

01:08:30.670 --> 01:08:33.490
felt I don't care what
environment you put me in,

01:08:33.490 --> 01:08:36.819
I am going to learn, and then
I'm going to become competent,

01:08:36.819 --> 01:08:38.500
and then I'm going to compete.

01:08:38.500 --> 01:08:43.210
My whole adult life I've been
a self-employed entrepreneur.

01:08:43.210 --> 01:08:47.560
I've built small companies
into medium-sized companies,

01:08:47.560 --> 01:08:50.260
and that entails being
everything from the CEO

01:08:50.260 --> 01:08:51.130
to the janitor.

01:08:51.130 --> 01:08:53.770
And I would say that
very much sums up

01:08:53.770 --> 01:08:56.380
my business experience,
my football experience,

01:08:56.380 --> 01:08:58.120
and my life experience.

01:08:58.120 --> 01:09:01.962
2006, I brought everybody in
a conference room and said,

01:09:01.962 --> 01:09:03.170
look, you guys are brilliant.

01:09:03.170 --> 01:09:03.939
I hired all of you.

01:09:03.939 --> 01:09:04.897
You're smarter than me.

01:09:04.897 --> 01:09:06.700
I'm going to go coach
high school football,

01:09:06.700 --> 01:09:08.020
you guys run the company.

01:09:08.020 --> 01:09:09.268
And they did a great job.

01:09:09.268 --> 01:09:10.810
I always tell
everybody I worked hard

01:09:10.810 --> 01:09:13.310
my whole life to getting back
to doing what I love the most.

01:09:16.500 --> 01:09:19.170
When I first started coaching,
it was like herding cats.

01:09:19.170 --> 01:09:26.550
If you want to be competitive in
high-level California football,

01:09:26.550 --> 01:09:29.910
it's 12 months of the year.

01:09:29.910 --> 01:09:31.710
We end our season,
we're in the weight room

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:33.540
the following Monday.

01:09:33.540 --> 01:09:36.370
And the parents stood up and
said, why are you doing this?

01:09:36.370 --> 01:09:38.760
I said, because it's hard.

01:09:38.760 --> 01:09:41.460
Because boys need hard.

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:43.350
Because life is hard.

01:09:43.350 --> 01:09:45.450
My first season we were 6 and 4.

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:49.170
In '18 we were 12 and 1.

01:09:49.170 --> 01:09:53.100
We've won the toughest league
in the county three times.

01:09:53.100 --> 01:09:55.290
The expectations are high.

01:09:55.290 --> 01:09:58.830
In March 2020, before
our spring break,

01:09:58.830 --> 01:10:02.640
we were told school would
be closed for two weeks.

01:10:02.640 --> 01:10:05.420
It ended up being
for over a year.

01:10:05.420 --> 01:10:10.820
I got more phone calls from kids
in trouble from March of 2020

01:10:10.820 --> 01:10:16.835
to March of 2021 than in all of
my previous 14 years combined

01:10:16.835 --> 01:10:20.980
and we were right there.

01:10:20.980 --> 01:10:23.920
Our kids, after a lift, I
was looking at the kids,

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:26.410
and I don't even know
what came over me,

01:10:26.410 --> 01:10:28.810
but I looked at the
kids, I said look,

01:10:28.810 --> 01:10:32.940
I'm probably going
to crash and burn,

01:10:32.940 --> 01:10:37.470
but I promise you I'm going
to do everything I can

01:10:37.470 --> 01:10:40.570
as your head coach
to get sports back.

01:10:40.570 --> 01:10:44.080
And that's how it started.

01:10:44.080 --> 01:10:46.030
SPEAKER 2: Local coaches,
parents and athletes

01:10:46.030 --> 01:10:47.205
say enough is enough.

01:10:47.205 --> 01:10:48.080
It's time for change.

01:10:48.080 --> 01:10:50.018
It's time to play sports again.

01:10:50.018 --> 01:10:52.060
And now they're holding
an event to get the state

01:10:52.060 --> 01:10:53.150
to pay attention.

01:10:53.150 --> 01:10:55.150
Head football coach at
Torrey Pines High School,

01:10:55.150 --> 01:10:55.720
Ron Gladnick.

01:10:55.720 --> 01:10:56.830
SPEAKER 3: Ron Gladnick.

01:10:56.830 --> 01:10:57.940
SPEAKER 4: Ron Gladnick.

01:10:57.940 --> 01:10:59.980
Coach, thank you for
once again joining us.

01:10:59.980 --> 01:11:01.022
RON GLADNICK: We started.

01:11:01.022 --> 01:11:04.300
We formed six SEAL teams
of parent volunteers

01:11:04.300 --> 01:11:06.190
who had different jobs.

01:11:06.190 --> 01:11:09.160
I was in charge of
SEAL Team Six, which

01:11:09.160 --> 01:11:11.440
was engaging our politicians.

01:11:11.440 --> 01:11:15.070
We started an app and we got
1,600 head high school football

01:11:15.070 --> 01:11:19.930
coaches in California in the
app and 7,000 assistant coaches.

01:11:19.930 --> 01:11:23.780
So 11 days after I started,
it was 4:30 in the afternoon,

01:11:23.780 --> 01:11:27.232
my cell phone rang and
it was Gavin Newsom.

01:11:27.232 --> 01:11:31.270
Him and I talked for 45 minutes,
and what I left him with

01:11:31.270 --> 01:11:32.770
was this.

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:34.900
I said, governor we
are not political.

01:11:34.900 --> 01:11:36.610
We do not want to be political.

01:11:36.610 --> 01:11:39.620
We want to do the
right thing for kids.

01:11:39.620 --> 01:11:43.490
He wanted sports
back, but the mosh pit

01:11:43.490 --> 01:11:46.430
of special interests that
you had to work through

01:11:46.430 --> 01:11:49.850
to get it back from
that day took five weeks

01:11:49.850 --> 01:11:53.655
until we finally got it done.

01:11:53.655 --> 01:11:56.280
And as a result, not only did we
get high school football back,

01:11:56.280 --> 01:11:59.250
but we got all high
school sports back.

01:11:59.250 --> 01:12:03.060
All seasons were played
from March to June.

01:12:03.060 --> 01:12:05.730
Youth sports were returned.

01:12:05.730 --> 01:12:08.235
It was incredible.

01:12:08.235 --> 01:12:13.210
3.3 million kids got to play.

01:12:13.210 --> 01:12:16.800
What could be better than that?

01:12:16.800 --> 01:12:19.200
At the end of the
day, our whole program

01:12:19.200 --> 01:12:22.040
here has nothing to
do with football.

01:12:22.040 --> 01:12:25.160
It has everything to do with
building great young men.

01:12:25.160 --> 01:12:27.260
And I believe if you do
that, the football will

01:12:27.260 --> 01:12:28.980
take care of itself.

01:12:28.980 --> 01:12:30.980
And I learned that
from Dick Lowry.

01:12:30.980 --> 01:12:34.430
This is stuff I learned
from my college head coach,

01:12:34.430 --> 01:12:38.520
my defensive coordinator.

01:12:38.520 --> 01:12:40.500
So for me it all started there.

01:12:43.979 --> 01:12:47.955
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:01.930 --> 01:13:02.680
Thank you so much.

01:13:06.400 --> 01:13:09.000
KERRY CUMMINGS: Ron, on behalf
of the Hillsdale College Alumni

01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:12.420
Association, we present you
with the Distinguished Award

01:13:12.420 --> 01:13:15.180
for Outstanding Achievement.

01:13:15.180 --> 01:13:18.550
And I'm going to have
to look directly--

01:13:22.300 --> 01:13:23.680
I'd forgotten the category.

01:13:23.680 --> 01:13:26.350
I lost my page.

01:13:26.350 --> 01:13:27.490
But congratulations.

01:13:27.490 --> 01:13:30.948
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:41.507 --> 01:13:43.090
RON GLADNICK: I've
limited my comments

01:13:43.090 --> 01:13:45.530
to two hours and
27 minutes, so--

01:13:45.530 --> 01:13:46.030
No.

01:13:49.650 --> 01:13:53.910
I've never been one to
reflect on a birthday.

01:13:53.910 --> 01:13:58.140
To me, you're the age
you feel and I've always

01:13:58.140 --> 01:14:01.980
tried to approach life
feeling young and vibrant and

01:14:01.980 --> 01:14:04.380
enthusiastic every single day.

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:06.990
But on September
12th I turned 60,

01:14:06.990 --> 01:14:10.350
and it's the first time
I've ever paused in my life

01:14:10.350 --> 01:14:16.180
and really reflected on how you
get to a certain place in time.

01:14:16.180 --> 01:14:19.180
And what was fascinating
about taking some time

01:14:19.180 --> 01:14:24.820
to do that is your whole
life is determined by really

01:14:24.820 --> 01:14:29.850
at the end of the day, a
few chance interactions that

01:14:29.850 --> 01:14:32.240
change the course of your life.

01:14:32.240 --> 01:14:35.330
And I was a young
kid back East getting

01:14:35.330 --> 01:14:38.150
ready to sign with Syracuse
University for football who

01:14:38.150 --> 01:14:39.830
was not very good.

01:14:39.830 --> 01:14:46.850
And John Servini entered my
life, came to recruit me.

01:14:46.850 --> 01:14:50.630
My parents are very
conservative, so John--

01:14:50.630 --> 01:14:53.190
I will never forget this, I've
told this story 100 times,

01:14:53.190 --> 01:14:55.460
he was a much bigger
guy back then--

01:14:55.460 --> 01:14:58.010
came into my living room and
sat with my mother and father.

01:14:58.010 --> 01:15:01.310
And he crossed his leg and
he balanced a coffee cup

01:15:01.310 --> 01:15:05.930
on a little tiny plate on his
thigh and talked to my parents

01:15:05.930 --> 01:15:06.530
for--

01:15:06.530 --> 01:15:11.060
I kept looking at the cup
going, it's a matter of time,

01:15:11.060 --> 01:15:14.120
but he never, the
guy never flinched.

01:15:14.120 --> 01:15:16.532
And I forget what day
of the week it was,

01:15:16.532 --> 01:15:18.740
but John came, I talked to
my parents, I went to bed,

01:15:18.740 --> 01:15:20.573
I woke up the next day,
my mom and dad said,

01:15:20.573 --> 01:15:23.030
you're going to
Hillsdale College.

01:15:23.030 --> 01:15:24.440
So that was my choice.

01:15:26.990 --> 01:15:30.470
And then I got here,
and for a kid back East

01:15:30.470 --> 01:15:32.240
coming to the Midwest
for the first time

01:15:32.240 --> 01:15:35.870
it was a lot of culture shock.

01:15:35.870 --> 01:15:37.250
And it was really different.

01:15:37.250 --> 01:15:39.440
And we were a terrible
football team.

01:15:39.440 --> 01:15:41.030
I mean really bad.

01:15:41.030 --> 01:15:42.440
I mean so bad.

01:15:42.440 --> 01:15:45.950
And there are people--

01:15:45.950 --> 01:15:49.340
So I always look back, and John
got me to Hillsdale College

01:15:49.340 --> 01:15:53.000
and Hillsdale College changed
the course of my whole life.

01:15:53.000 --> 01:15:58.010
And then John Wilson, my
advisor, is John here?

01:15:58.010 --> 01:15:59.494
Stand up John.

01:15:59.494 --> 01:16:02.952
[APPLAUSE]

01:16:07.400 --> 01:16:09.800
My first two years here, John
Wilson kept me in school.

01:16:12.950 --> 01:16:15.410
Was not only a
mentor and my advisor

01:16:15.410 --> 01:16:21.450
but was a friend, a friend who
cared about you personally,

01:16:21.450 --> 01:16:25.710
well beyond the scope of his job
as a professor and an advisor.

01:16:25.710 --> 01:16:29.310
And there were some bumpy
moments in that first two

01:16:29.310 --> 01:16:34.080
years, and John was always a
calming influence and always

01:16:34.080 --> 01:16:36.570
was the guy, hey,
it's going to be OK

01:16:36.570 --> 01:16:38.110
and we'll move on
to the next thing.

01:16:38.110 --> 01:16:41.280
And so, John, you changed
the course of my life

01:16:41.280 --> 01:16:45.390
by being a good friend and
being a great history professor

01:16:45.390 --> 01:16:46.695
and I appreciate you very much.

01:16:49.360 --> 01:16:52.215
My mom and dad are
too frail to travel,

01:16:52.215 --> 01:16:54.340
but they're watching tonight,
along with my family.

01:16:54.340 --> 01:16:56.925
And I want to thank them all.

01:16:56.925 --> 01:16:58.800
I want to thank my mom
and dad for raising us

01:16:58.800 --> 01:17:02.730
right, my brother and sister and
I. And I look at our families

01:17:02.730 --> 01:17:05.565
now and we spend a lot of
time together and none of that

01:17:05.565 --> 01:17:06.940
would have happened
without them.

01:17:06.940 --> 01:17:08.730
So they changed the
course of my life.

01:17:12.690 --> 01:17:15.900
One of the beautiful things
about Hillsdale for me

01:17:15.900 --> 01:17:20.940
is that ability to be put in
any environment and thrive.

01:17:20.940 --> 01:17:26.240
And I don't know that people
value that skill set enough

01:17:26.240 --> 01:17:29.870
anymore, but it's the
most important thing

01:17:29.870 --> 01:17:31.960
this college does.

01:17:31.960 --> 01:17:34.618
Because I look at
Jared, I look at you,

01:17:34.618 --> 01:17:36.910
I look at all the people that
have spoken here tonight,

01:17:36.910 --> 01:17:40.840
and I get the sense you can
be put in any environment

01:17:40.840 --> 01:17:41.770
and succeed.

01:17:41.770 --> 01:17:45.370
Now Jared I'm older than you,
so my bones creak and stuff,

01:17:45.370 --> 01:17:48.310
so I did want to put my
head on your body for a year

01:17:48.310 --> 01:17:51.850
and your head on my body so
you can get a preview of what

01:17:51.850 --> 01:17:53.650
the future holds.

01:17:53.650 --> 01:17:54.355
It's a gift.

01:17:57.340 --> 01:18:00.220
I'd like to Thank Jeff
Lantis, because Jeff

01:18:00.220 --> 01:18:03.490
does an amazing job of keeping
guys like me who are far

01:18:03.490 --> 01:18:05.590
away connected to the college.

01:18:05.590 --> 01:18:10.960
And his impact on my
tie to Hillsdale College

01:18:10.960 --> 01:18:13.960
has been very, very dramatic.

01:18:13.960 --> 01:18:17.630
At the end of the
day Coach Lowry

01:18:17.630 --> 01:18:21.230
and Coach Dye, the
professors and the courses

01:18:21.230 --> 01:18:23.810
and all the things
that we do here,

01:18:23.810 --> 01:18:27.020
again, gave me
the gift to learn.

01:18:27.020 --> 01:18:30.980
It was the
organizational efficiency

01:18:30.980 --> 01:18:35.820
that Dick Lowry and
Dave Dye taught me

01:18:35.820 --> 01:18:40.530
that shaped my business
career, because it was hard.

01:18:40.530 --> 01:18:43.440
They treated people with
dignity and respect,

01:18:43.440 --> 01:18:46.770
but then they empowered
us to be successful.

01:18:46.770 --> 01:18:50.500
And that's the cornerstone
of every single business

01:18:50.500 --> 01:18:52.360
I've ever run.

01:18:52.360 --> 01:18:53.770
Treat people right.

01:18:53.770 --> 01:18:55.660
We had 500 employees.

01:18:55.660 --> 01:18:58.683
No one ever punched a
clock in the company, ever.

01:18:58.683 --> 01:19:00.600
You had to go to a
baseball game for your kid,

01:19:00.600 --> 01:19:02.010
go to a baseball game.

01:19:02.010 --> 01:19:04.710
You need it to be an adult and
make decisions about your time,

01:19:04.710 --> 01:19:06.840
go ahead, just get
your work done.

01:19:06.840 --> 01:19:09.390
And I was always
so proud I would

01:19:09.390 --> 01:19:11.400
drive by the facility
at 11 o'clock at night

01:19:11.400 --> 01:19:13.230
and there'd be 20 lights
on in the building

01:19:13.230 --> 01:19:16.200
because people did the right
thing when given the choice

01:19:16.200 --> 01:19:17.460
to do the right thing.

01:19:17.460 --> 01:19:21.390
And my experience with the
football team or a company,

01:19:21.390 --> 01:19:23.400
a group of adults or
a group of teenagers,

01:19:23.400 --> 01:19:26.880
is if you treat them
right, and you love them,

01:19:26.880 --> 01:19:28.630
and you look out for
their best interests,

01:19:28.630 --> 01:19:32.430
and they know you do, the
lion's share of people

01:19:32.430 --> 01:19:35.230
will do the right thing
every single time.

01:19:35.230 --> 01:19:43.530
And if I can leave you with
one thought it would be this--

01:19:43.530 --> 01:19:47.670
be humble, work
hard, do the best

01:19:47.670 --> 01:19:49.740
you can, and at
the end of the day,

01:19:49.740 --> 01:19:51.210
when your work is
done, hopefully

01:19:51.210 --> 01:19:54.760
we'll leave the Earth in a
better place than we found it.

01:19:54.760 --> 01:19:58.920
And if you do that, like me,
because of Hillsdale College,

01:19:58.920 --> 01:20:01.420
you too will have
a beautiful life.

01:20:01.420 --> 01:20:02.520
I thank you so much.

01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:05.590
This is such a humbling
experience for me.

01:20:05.590 --> 01:20:07.120
I love being back here.

01:20:07.120 --> 01:20:09.670
Fills my soul, with
all the good stuff.

01:20:09.670 --> 01:20:10.760
Thank you, Dr. Arnn.

01:20:10.760 --> 01:20:12.910
And Dr. Arnn, thank
you for continuously

01:20:12.910 --> 01:20:14.920
raising the academic
standards so

01:20:14.920 --> 01:20:16.547
that when I'm out
in the world and I

01:20:16.547 --> 01:20:18.130
say I went to Hillsdale
College, well,

01:20:18.130 --> 01:20:24.400
that's just like the Ivy League,
and I go, yes, yes it is.

01:20:24.400 --> 01:20:26.560
So thank you, sir.

01:20:26.560 --> 01:20:27.310
God bless.

01:20:27.310 --> 01:20:28.300
Thank you very much.

01:20:28.300 --> 01:20:31.751
[APPLAUSE]

01:20:44.570 --> 01:20:46.850
LARRY ARNN: I forgot
to thank three people.

01:20:46.850 --> 01:20:49.530
But coming up here gives me
a chance to say two things.

01:20:49.530 --> 01:20:54.377
One is I'm glad Joy got
the award because otherwise

01:20:54.377 --> 01:20:56.960
the entire field would have been
a much better football player

01:20:56.960 --> 01:20:58.680
than I ever was.

01:20:58.680 --> 01:21:01.700
And also the kids don't
actually walk around

01:21:01.700 --> 01:21:08.420
in slow motion on
the college campus,

01:21:08.420 --> 01:21:09.740
walking through molasses.

01:21:09.740 --> 01:21:13.760
Who I forgot to thank are
Maryann Rotale and Kerry

01:21:13.760 --> 01:21:16.700
Cummings, who've been just
tremendous in this job.

01:21:16.700 --> 01:21:19.760
And the alumni board
is growing all the time

01:21:19.760 --> 01:21:23.450
and it's a blessing to the
college and I'm grateful.

01:21:23.450 --> 01:21:26.450
And of course Colleen
is excellent at the job.

01:21:26.450 --> 01:21:29.761
[APPLAUSE]

01:21:33.570 --> 01:21:35.820
And I don't know if Neil
Cole is going to be any good,

01:21:35.820 --> 01:21:37.972
but we have our fingers crossed.

01:21:37.972 --> 01:21:39.180
Thank you all for being here.

01:21:47.190 --> 01:21:48.920
KERRY CUMMINGS:
Thank you, Dr. Arnn.

01:21:48.920 --> 01:21:50.970
Such inspiring stories tonight.

01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:54.680
It truly was an honor to
be among these awardees.

01:21:54.680 --> 01:21:57.920
And this concludes the
presentation of our awards.

01:21:57.920 --> 01:21:59.725
Congratulations to all of you.

01:21:59.725 --> 01:22:01.100
And at this time,
I'd like to ask

01:22:01.100 --> 01:22:03.785
Colleen to come to the podium
for a few closing remarks.

01:22:06.046 --> 01:22:09.399
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:10.840 --> 01:22:12.460
COLLEEN MCGINNESS: Thank you.

01:22:12.460 --> 01:22:15.510
Well, we have a party
planned afterward

01:22:15.510 --> 01:22:16.470
and I know you're all--

01:22:16.470 --> 01:22:18.270
I hope you're all,
anxious to get to it.

01:22:18.270 --> 01:22:20.370
I hope it becomes a fun night--

01:22:20.370 --> 01:22:22.410
not that it isn't
already, but we

01:22:22.410 --> 01:22:25.950
have the Hillcats faculty jazz
band who played earlier for us.

01:22:25.950 --> 01:22:28.140
They're going to continue
on for a couple of hours.

01:22:28.140 --> 01:22:30.420
And we have quite
a few alumni who

01:22:30.420 --> 01:22:34.200
are out enjoying a reception
who are going to join and merge

01:22:34.200 --> 01:22:38.820
with us for a decades party,
so I hope you'll stay for that.

01:22:38.820 --> 01:22:41.280
I have a few more
housekeeping announcements

01:22:41.280 --> 01:22:42.030
before we adjourn.

01:22:44.387 --> 01:22:46.470
I want to run through a
little bit of what's going

01:22:46.470 --> 01:22:48.450
on tomorrow just as a reminder.

01:22:48.450 --> 01:22:52.110
But we have the It's The
Pancakes pancake brunch

01:22:52.110 --> 01:22:55.210
outside of the chapel
tomorrow from 9:00 to 11:00

01:22:55.210 --> 01:22:58.200
in front of Christ Chapel.

01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:00.420
The alumni choir will be
practicing with the music

01:23:00.420 --> 01:23:03.150
program in the chapel and
will have a brief performance

01:23:03.150 --> 01:23:05.130
at noon in Christ Chapel.

01:23:05.130 --> 01:23:07.590
The alumni tailgate will
be from noon to 4:00

01:23:07.590 --> 01:23:09.330
over by the Sports
Complex and will

01:23:09.330 --> 01:23:13.170
feature food, of course beer,
and music from alumna Nicole

01:23:13.170 --> 01:23:15.510
Corey's band Arctic Clam.

01:23:15.510 --> 01:23:18.480
The football game begins at
4:00 and the first 100 people

01:23:18.480 --> 01:23:21.090
to check in at the alumni
tent will receive a discounted

01:23:21.090 --> 01:23:21.930
ticket.

01:23:21.930 --> 01:23:24.120
Following the game, we'll
be hosting a fifth quarter

01:23:24.120 --> 01:23:25.950
reception in the
president's suite

01:23:25.950 --> 01:23:28.938
which is inside the Sports
Complex, where we'll

01:23:28.938 --> 01:23:30.480
be able to watch
some of the students

01:23:30.480 --> 01:23:33.780
mock rock dances if you're
into that kind of thing.

01:23:33.780 --> 01:23:36.450
I want to thank Bon Appetit and
the Searle Center team for all

01:23:36.450 --> 01:23:38.340
their good work, as well
as the alumni office

01:23:38.340 --> 01:23:40.980
team for their effort putting
together this weekend.

01:23:40.980 --> 01:23:44.670
I want to particularly
thank Ashley Sallows, who

01:23:44.670 --> 01:23:47.700
was the lead planner for all
of our homecoming festivities.

01:23:47.700 --> 01:23:49.170
She's worked tirelessly.

01:23:49.170 --> 01:23:49.920
Thank you, Ashley.

01:23:49.920 --> 01:23:53.142
[APPLAUSE]

01:23:56.070 --> 01:23:59.400
I'd like to think Dr.
Arnn and John Servini

01:23:59.400 --> 01:24:02.530
for supporting our
alumni outreach ventures

01:24:02.530 --> 01:24:04.550
and the alumni office.

01:24:04.550 --> 01:24:07.050
Our alumni are a very important
part of the Hillsdale family

01:24:07.050 --> 01:24:08.717
and spending time
together will continue

01:24:08.717 --> 01:24:10.600
to build that relationship.

01:24:10.600 --> 01:24:14.250
So I want you to mark your
calendars for May 19 to 22,

01:24:14.250 --> 01:24:16.980
where we will host our second
annual White and Blue Weekend.

01:24:16.980 --> 01:24:18.750
And it's a weekend
just for alumni.

01:24:18.750 --> 01:24:21.120
If you haven't heard of
it, we had our first White

01:24:21.120 --> 01:24:22.620
and Blue weekend this last May.

01:24:22.620 --> 01:24:23.492
It was a blast.

01:24:23.492 --> 01:24:25.200
There's something for
everyone, including

01:24:25.200 --> 01:24:27.090
your kids if you
have them, and lots

01:24:27.090 --> 01:24:29.910
of room on campus for alumni
to take over and enjoy campus

01:24:29.910 --> 01:24:31.600
and academic life once again.

01:24:31.600 --> 01:24:34.290
So please consider joining
us for that this year

01:24:34.290 --> 01:24:38.130
if you didn't last here and
also if you did, come back.

01:24:38.130 --> 01:24:42.390
It's been such a joy to
welcome you all back home.

01:24:42.390 --> 01:24:44.740
I'm always very inspired
by this evening.

01:24:44.740 --> 01:24:46.200
It's my favorite
night of the year.

01:24:46.200 --> 01:24:50.580
And it's really fun to spotlight
and elevate our alumni.

01:24:50.580 --> 01:24:52.410
They're just
fantastic ambassadors

01:24:52.410 --> 01:24:53.620
of what Hillsdale does here.

01:24:53.620 --> 01:24:55.080
So I'm very proud to do that.

01:24:55.080 --> 01:24:57.450
As you know, there's
no place like Hillsdale

01:24:57.450 --> 01:24:59.530
and we need the college
now more than ever.

01:24:59.530 --> 01:25:02.050
Thank you so much and enjoy
the rest of your evening.

01:25:02.050 --> 01:25:05.400
[APPLAUSE]

